\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE-UPDATE 2-Indonesia makes surprise rate cut to counter global risks\b0\par\par\uc2 1,134 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2011\par 13:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Fixes final bullet point)\par \par \uc2 * Bank Indonesia cuts rate by 25 bps to 6.5 pct\par \par \uc2 * Cbank gov says cut to anticipate global slowdown\par \par \uc2 * Exports seen slowing in 2012, gov says\par \par \uc2 * Cbank says it will keep guarding rupiah\par \par \uc2 By Aditya Suharmoko and Neil Chatterjee\par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Oct 11 (Reuters) - Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly cut its benchmark policy rate on Tuesday, the first such move by a G20 member after Brazil, as Jakarta seeks to stimulate domestic demand and maintain investor confidence in Southeast Asia's biggest economy.\par \par \uc2 Despite few signs of actual cooling in a hot Indonesian consumer market, Bank Indonesia cut the rate by 25 basis points to a record low 6.50 percent, underscoring growing concern among policymakers that a global recession may be coming.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia may also have started an easing cycle to keep international portfolio investors onboard, after a slide in its bonds and stocks in September on global risk aversion, as policymakers fear a repeat of destabilising selloffs.\par \par \uc2 "So far, the impact of the global economic turmoil is more felt in the financial markets, while the real sector is not yet impacted. However, a weakening global economy is estimated to impact the domestic economy in 2012," said Bank Indonesia in Tuesday's policy statement, adding this would lead it to pursue interest rate and other monetary policy responses.\par \par \uc2 "We are bringing the policy rate to a level that is more reasonable," Governor Darmin Nasution told reporters. "We saw the 6.75 percent rate as too high, unless we estimated inflation next year to be very high."\par \par \uc2 Just a month ago, many analysts expected Bank Indonesia -- which only raised rate once this year, in February -- to hike them once more by end-2011 to curb inflation. But then, as turmoil wracked financial markets while inflation eased, , \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials started talking about having "room" to cut rates.\par \par \uc2 So Bank Indonesia was seen as being among the first Asian central banks that would loosen policy. Still, a cut as early as now was not expected. In a Reuters poll last week, all 11 analysts forecast that BI would leave the rate at 6.75 percent on Tuesday, though three of the 11 forecast a cut to 6.50 percent by the year-end.\par \par \uc2 In September, annual inflation slowed to 4.61 percent due to easing food prices, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  now sees it below 5 percent next year too, well within its policy target range.\par \par \uc2 As global funds saw Europe's debt crisis intensify, they pulled money out of Indonesian stocks and bonds, reinforcing Bank Indonesia's worries about the country's exposure to foreign funds and leading policymakers to keep asserting that the economy's domestic fundamentals are in good shape.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, industry data on Tuesday showed car sales in September surged 62 percent from a year earlier, while motorbike sales jumped 51 percent. Toyota last month announced plans for $337 million of investment in the country.\par \par \uc2 Yet foreign portfolio investors, who held 35.7 percent of total Indonesian government bonds in early September, dumped 37.13 trillion rupiah by Oct 5, reducing their ownership to 30.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's rate cut led the yield on benchmark 10-year Indonesian government bonds to edge lower to 6.4 percent on investor buying, from 6.5 percent before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENING\b0 \highlight\uc2  HEAVILY\par \par \uc2 BI has more than doubled its foreign reserves since 2008 and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the past month to prop up the rupiah, causing the reserves to fall to $114.5 billion as of Sept. 30 from $124.6 billion a month earlier.\par \par \uc2 Still, the rupiah has slid 5 percent since the end of August, undermining BI's efforts to use a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temper imported inflation and leaving reserves likely to fall further as Nasution said BI will continue guarding the rupiah.\par \par \uc2 "This is a brave decision. Two thumbs up. This is not expected by the market and it shows that Bank Indonesia is over confident with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the short term, the rupiah will depreciate...Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will need to do more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Juniman, economist at BII in Jakarta.\par \par \uc2 "Inflation is below 5 percent, that's why they cut the rate...In the medium and long term, the decision will give stimulus to domestic demand."\par \par \uc2 The rupiah was little changed after the rate decision, having earlier fallen about 0.6 percent against the dollar. Indonesian stocks also showed little initial reaction to the rate move, holding on to gains of more than 2 percent earlier in the session, buoyed by global markets.\par \par \uc2 " The key will be whether this move encourages equity inflows back into the economy on the basis that Asia is reflating," said Jonathan Cavenagh, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 HIGHER LENDING AHEAD?\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's economy is expected to post solid 6.6 percent growth this year as exports remain strong in spite of a global slowdown. The country had annual growth of 6.5 percent in the first half on buoyant household consumption and investment.\par \par \uc2 Nasution said full-year loan growth was seen holding up at 24-25 percent, a figure bank executives expected to be boosted by its latest move and the possibility of a further cut.\par \par \uc2 "If inflation is manageable, the BI rate still has room to decline. The exchange rate is manageable and credit demand will be bigger," said Jahja Setiatmadja, CEO of Bank Central Asia\par \par \uc2 , Indonesia's biggest lender by market value.\par \par \uc2 A consumer confidence index rose in September as consumers saw lower financing costs and slower inflation in coming months\par \par \uc2 . Nasution said on Tuesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  saw domestic consumption and investment staying strong in 2012, though exports were likely to slow on weaker global demand.\par \par \uc2 The country's export growth slowed in August, but at 37 percent from a year earlier would still be in the envy of many. The country is the leading exporter of thermal coal for power, palm oil and tin.\par \par \uc2 Economists say Indonesia is in better shape to face a global crisis than it was in 2008 and will likely expand above 6 percent next year even with an international slowdown.\par \par \uc2 "While this might be authorities' attempt at pre-emptively cushioning downside risks to growth, we still believe that the move was a tad premature given the need to support rupiah and contain volatility in the local markets. Strong loan growth and flush liquidity conditions could pose price risks going forward," said Radhika Rao, economist at Forecast in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Adriana Nina Kusuma, Rieka Rahadiana and Fathiya Dahrul in JAKARTA,; Razak Ahmad in KUALA LUMPUR and Karen Lema in MANILA; Editing by Richard Borsuk)\par \par \uc2 INDONESIA-ECONOMY/RATE (UPDATE 2, PIX CORRECTED)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111014e7ab000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
