\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 934 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2011\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ LEAVES OVERNIGHT CALL RATE RANGE AT 0.0% TO 0.1% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan on Friday kept its monetary policy on hold, opting to proceed with its easing steps taken in August as the strong yen and uncertainty about the health of the global economy have yet to derail the nation's recovery. (published 0401 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FINAL SENATE VOTE ON CHINESE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  BILL LIKELY DELAYED UNTIL NEXT WEEK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A final vote on legislation that would direct the Obama administration to take action against China's alleged manipulation of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  value was likely delayed until next week, after an unusual argument erupted on the Senate floor between the party leaders Thursday evening. (published 2345 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GREENSPAN: TO STAY VIABLE, EURO MAY NEED POLITICAL UNION -FT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A full-fledged political union might be needed for the euro zone to sustain its common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and overcome divisions that sometimes reward less-responsible members, former U.S. Federal Reserve chairman Alan Greenspan said. (published 2249 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S&P DOWNGRADES DEXIA CORE BANKS ON CONSTRAINTS TO WHOLESALE FUNDING ACCESS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ratings firm Standard's & Poor's Friday lowered its credit rating on Franco-Belgian bank \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dexia S.A\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., saying it believes the bank is having trouble accessing wholesale funding markets. (published 0124 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: LEAKED DRAFT OF 'VOLCKER RULE' HITS INTERNET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Billions of dollars are at stake for big banks, which have been working for months to shape the rule aimed at curbing risky trading activities that played a part in the financial crisis. (published 2359 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: BOK GOV: 2011 GDP GROWTH LIKELY LOWER THAN 4.3% FORECAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Korea Gov. Kim Choong-soo said Friday he expects the country's economic growth this year to come below the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's projection for a 4.3% rise, in his clearest indication yet that the BOK will cut its growth forecast for Asia's fourth-largest economy. (published 0410 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA FINANCE MINISTER: SEE DOWNSIDE RISKS TO 2012 GROWTH FORECAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan Friday acknowledged that there are downside risks to the government's forecast for 4.5% economic growth next year. (published 0301 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: IMF SAYS RISKS TO AUSTRALIA OUTLOOK TILTED TO DOWNSIDE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Recent market volatility and a slowing in global growth means the risks to an otherwise robust outlook for Australia's economy are now tilted to the downside, the International Monetary Fund said Friday. (published 0454 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN LABOR MARKET PRESSURES STILL A THREAT - IMF \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The International Monetary Fund Friday warned that labor market pressures could yet emerge in Australia, especially in mining states where conditions are already drum tight. (published 0400 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: SWISS SEPTEMBER UNEMPLOYMENT RATE HOLDS AT 2.8% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Switzerland's unemployment rate held at its lowest level in almost three years in September, as solid economic growth continued to support hiring, data released Friday showed. (published 0545 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: MALAYSIA AUGUST EXPORTS +10.9%; +9.4% EXPECTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's exports grew a stronger-than-expected 10.9% on-year in August, helped by a sharp increase in shipments of palm oil, chemicals and chemical products, liquefied natural gas and rubber products. (published 0401 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK INDONESIA DEPUTY GOV: EXPECT 'CONCRETE MEASURES' TO BOOST MARKET CONFIDENCE IN NEXT G-20 MEET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia expects "concrete measures" from a Group of 20 meeting of finance ministers and other officials from developed and developing economies next week, which will help boost market confidence, Deputy Gov. Hartadi Sarwono said Friday. (published 0528 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDONESIA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RESERVES FALL TO $114.5B IN SEPT VS $124.6B IN AUG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves fell to $114.5 billion as of Sept. 30 from $124.6 billion a month earlier as the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to halt the rupiah's decline, data from Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  showed Friday. (published 0137 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: THAI FINANCE MINISTER CRITICIZES MONETARY POLICY FOR SLOWING ECONOMY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thai Finance Minister Thirachai Phuvanatnaranubala Friday criticized the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Monetary Policy Committee, saying it has put the brakes on the local economy too much and too soon. (published 0519 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PHILIPPINES CANCELS 10-YEAR BOND AUCTION TUES; MAKES WAY FOR RETAIL BOND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippines has cancelled an auction of 10-year treasury bonds scheduled for Tuesday to make way for the price-setting auction of retail treasury bonds, the National Treasury said on its website Friday. (published 0012 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA'S ZHEJIANG PROVINCE TO ISSUE CNY8 BLN BONDS - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government of China's eastern Zhejiang province will issue CNY8 billion worth of bonds to fund infrastructure projects this year, as part of a trial that allows local governments to sell bonds directly, the state-run China Daily reported Thursday, without offering a timetable for the sale. (published 0250 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par WSJ(10/7) HEARD ON THE STREET: BOE, ECB COULD USE POLICY SWAP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unconventional policy is becoming familiar territory. The Bank of England and the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  both left rates on hold Thursday. But both are deeply worried about the effects of the euro-zone sovereign-debt crisis on the real economy. The BOE plans to buy another GBP 75 billion of government bonds, while the ECB is providing more support to banks. They could do better if they swapped policies. (published 2319 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(10/7) HEARD ON THE ST: EURO SLUMP TURNS UP HEAT ON JAPAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan Inc. is dealing with its dollar exposure. But the euro is another story. (published 2326 GMT) [ 07-10-11 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111007e7a7000al\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
