\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Bulls Temper Their Outlook With Caution\b0\par\par\uc2 830 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2011\par 11:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- Investors, speculators still long Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but holding back a while\par \par \uc2 -- More declines possible since Europe debt problem hasn't gone away\par \par \uc2 -- Singapore dollar, won may have been oversold in September\par \par \uc2 -- Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  declines mostly on hedging, not selling of underlying assets \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Martin Vaughan \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Investors and speculators remain bullish on Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , despite the shellacking the region's markets have taken this month, but they are hesitating to resume their purchases as they watch to see how the euro-zone debt crisis plays out.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some banks and fund managers say they are preparing to buy the Singapore dollar but are holding back for at least several weeks from buying other battered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , like the South Korean won, against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the global turmoil continues to threaten Asian and other emerging-market investments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I cannot rule out another 5% to 10% weakening of the major Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the next month or so," said Adam Gilmour, managing director and head of Asian corporate sales at Citibank. "But I have a very firm view on this--sell the dollar rally against Asia. When the dust settles, we'll see dollar/Asia go back to levels well below what we see now."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September's Asia-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rout has been dramatic: As recently as early August, some central banks were \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slow their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ' ascent; now many--Korea, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Taiwan, Thailand, Malaysia, the Philippines--have been in the market to defend them. The won has slid nearly 10% in September against the U.S. dollar and the Singapore dollar 7%, leading the region's decline and each shedding a year's worth of gains with a few weeks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We still want to be overweight on Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but over the next two months things are going to move around a lot more," said Endre Pedersen, managing director at Manulife Asset Management. "We're running less risk exposure than in a more stable market."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An exception may be the Singapore dollar. The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which had sunk from January's high above S$1.55 to a record low below S$1.20 in August, has since rebounded to S$1.2971. That steep pullback in the Singapore dollar is making it look attractive to some.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We had been short on the Sing dollar for quite some time, but at these levels we are looking for ways to get back in," Pedersen said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippine peso, by contrast, declined less than its regional peers, falling a comparatively small 3% this month. That is partly because foreign investors weren't as heavily positioned in the Philippines, and so there weren't as many trades to unwind. But it could also signal more room to catch up on the downside.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Much will depend on whether European policy makers can restore confidence that Greece won't suffer a disorderly default and that the region's banks can withstand any stresses. Market players say there hasn't yet been a broad sell-off in Asian emerging market debt--despite some commentators throwing the word "capitulation" around. Instead, the month's sharp declines in Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were driven by investors hedging the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk on their long-Asia bets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is almost no sign that real money has actually fled local markets," \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Jonathan Anderson wrote in a note to clients. "Rather, as best we can tell the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves are due primarily to panicked hedging by foreign investors together with the normal unwinding of hedged trades on the back of it."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign money has poured into emerging-market bonds and equities over the past two years, and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation trend was so well established that investors didn't bother to hedge many of these positions, seeing it as a further boost to their returns.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Most of them probably were entirely unhedged," said Ilan Solot, a strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London. "You give back a lot of carry trying to anticipate those kinds of risks." Investors seeking the high yields that emerging markets provide have been focused on maximizing those yields, and until the recent shocks have been hesitant to hedge them, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But that also means there is much more downside for Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if global conditions worsen beyond current expectations and foreign investors start dumping assets in earnest.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors will also be watching central banks closely for policy cues. Monetary tightening helped fuel Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ' long rally, and a shift to more accommodative policy to support growth could help them back down.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If investors get the faintest whiff that an Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will ease, that's going to set alarms off," said Selena Ling, head strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 OCBC Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Martin Vaughan, Dow Jones Newswires; +65 6415 4033; martin.vaughan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 30, 2011 05:29 ET (09:29 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110930e79u002ip\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
