\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Crude May Find Support At $79.76/Bbl -\uc2 MF Global\b0\par\par\uc2 1,670 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2011\par 04:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0220 GMT [Dow Jones] Continued disappointment with the stimulus solution unveiled by the U.S. Fed Wednesday will likely take a toll on energy prices, with Nymex crude futures expected to find support at $79.76/bbl, \f28 \uc2 \f2 MF Global\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Tom Pawlicki says in a note. "The oil market should maintain its downward direction in the near term, until risk markets can find a new level of stabilization." Factors weighing on oil markets include the Fed's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Twist reprise, dollar strength, continued uncertainty over Greek debt and cuts to the IMF's global growth estimates, Pawlicki says. Nymex November crude futures are at $81.22 on Globex, up 71 cents, while November ICE Brent crude is 74 cents higher at $106.23/bbl. (cheeyew.cheang@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0219 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares are down 2.3% at 438.91 in thin volume, with investors selling blue-chip stocks across the board following heavy falls on Wall Street, traders say. "Sentiment is falling after major global markets suffered big losses yesterday and more declines are expected today," says a trader with Saigon Securities. He notes that local sentiment is also weakened because foreigners are net sellers of more than VND700 billion of stocks so far this month. Among decliners, Masan is down 4.5% at VND127,300, \uc2 Bao Viet Holdings\uc2  is down 3.4% at VND68,000 and Vincom(VIC.VH) is down 3.8% at VND100,000. Traders tip immediate support for the key index at 435. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY rises after Friday's central parity is set higher at 6.3840 from Thursday's 6.3808. In OTC trade, the USD/CNY is at 6.3991 from 6.3877 late Thursday, and is tipped to face immediate resistance at Thursday's intraday high of 6.4025, says a Shanghai-based local bank trader. "The pair is mainly driven by a strong dollar amid unabated concerns over the euro zone debt woes," the trader says; he adds the increase in the fixing doesn't match the surge in the USD offshore, which is what the PBOC used to do over the past few months. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are up at 6.4410/6.4510 from 6.3990/6.4040 late Thursday, surging for the second straight session. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] Markets may become increasingly wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the USD/JPY tests the 76.00 floor, OCBC says in a note. "Look for the USD/JPY to test the 76.00 floor if risk appetite continues to turn rancid, although markets may grow increasingly wary of the MOF/BOJ," it says. It adds that there is a risk that there will be a snapback in risk appetite should, for instance, G20 leaders commit to buying eurozone periphery debt, or European governments announce TARP equivalent funds to buy bank's bad assets and/or to recapitalize banks. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is lower at 8,670 from 8,750 late Thursday on suspected Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the pair is off an earlier low at 8,650 as the market remains jittery following yesterday's massive selloff in Indonesia stocks, which fell nearly 9%, the largest one-day drop in more than three years. Three dealers suspect Bank Indonesia to have sold total $30 million so far today, and they expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to continue supplying the market with dollars later today. "The dollar buyers so far this morning are local companies. I don't hear foreign names buying the dollar yet," a dealer with a foreign name says. They tip a wide 8,650-8,950 range for the day. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI's 2.8% decline, again like in recent sessions, fails to capture the much steeper declines in some Hong Kong sectors; underperformers include commodity-related stocks, with blue chips Chalco (2600.HK), China Coal (1898.HK) and \uc2 Citic Pacific\uc2  (0267.HK) all falling by over 6.0%. Hong Kong retail stocks are taking a beating as well with Luk Fook (0590.HK), Chow Sang Sang Sang (0116.HK), Oriental Watch (0398.HK) and \uc2 Emperor Watch & Jewellery\uc2  (0887.HK) all falling by over 10%. Even the more defensive HK telecom stocks are slumping with SmarTone (0315.HK) plunging 9.2% to HK$12.44 on top of its 17.5% selloff Thursdays. "It's a bit overdone and probably over-discounting the deterioration in fundamentals," says \uc2 Steven Leung\uc2 , director of institutional sales at UOB KayHian. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0209 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNH, or offshore USD/CNY, rises sharply to a 3-month high in an extremely volatile morning of trade so far, despite steady onshore fixing and spot trade. Amid risk aversion, the offshore pair is last at 6.4995, extending gains from 6.4550 earlier, and up almost 600 pips from 6.4400 late Thursday. "The bid-offer spreads are huge, and the pair fluctuates a lot almost in every other second," a trader at a Chinese bank says, adding this is because poor market sentiment persists after euro weakness overnight and a soft reading on manufacturing activity in China on Thursday. She tips the pair to be biased up, but "it's hard to make a tip on the level" amid the current volatility. The pair is trading at a record premium of almost 1000 points to onshore spot. Onshore, the USD/CNY is at 6.3959, from 6.3877 late Thursday, after a stable fixing at 6.3840 vs 6.3808 Thursday. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares decline to a one-year low, in line with the retreat in overseas bourses amid heightened global economic fears. The benchmark PSEi is down 3.9% at 3935.20, led by a 7.0% retreat in mining & oil sub-indices; all other sub-indices are in the red with 122 decliners and only 7 advancers, while 14 issues are unchanged. Today's sell-off brings the market's decline since the start of the month to 9.8%. Jose Vistan, research head at AB Capital, tips next support at 3850, with resistance at 4160. Among leading decliners, PLDT (TEL.PH) is off 2.5% at PHP2,182, Lepanto Mining (LC.PH) is off 4.6% at PHP1.23 and Metropolitan Bank (MBT.PH) is off 3.9% at PHP65.20. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] The G-20 meeting is unlikely to yield any significant announcements that will see an increase in risk appetite that will cause a broad-based USD selloff, says Nick Verdi, Barclay's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Asia-Pacific ex-Japan. "I would expect little to come out from G-20. Probably they may make a commitment to free trade and anything beyond that in terms of coordinated action would be a positive surprise." He expects the USD to play the role of a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the crisis in the euro zone is resolved. He tips the EUR/USD to fall to the 1.250 level in three months. The pair is now at 1.3536. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open down, tracking sharp losses in regional bourses and Wall Street's decline overnight, though investors are hoping that some government support may lift market confidence; a local media reports China's national pension fund has received regulatory approval to invest CNY10 billion in the stock market. The Shanghai Composite Index is down 1.3% at 2412.43; support is tipped at 2400. "The CNY10 billion injection will help, but it isn't enough to offset the 3.5% fall in the Dow Jones Industrial Average. The pension fund definitely has more gunpowder in its reserves," says Wang Weijun, an analyst at Zheshang Securities. Among the most actively traded stocks, \uc2 Ping An Insurance\uc2  (Group) of China (601318.SH) is off 2.3% at CNY38.01, and \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (601857.SH) is down 0.9% at CNY9.51. The Shenzhen Composite Index is down 1.9% at 1050.39.(esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares are likely to open lower, weighed by declines in most Asian markets after stocks in the U.S. plunged Thursday amid concerns over the state of the U.S. economy, traders say. They expect the main index to find support at 3250 after it ended down 8.9% Thursday at 3369.143. "I expect, however, that the sell-off today won't be as huge as Thursday's as our fundamental economy is stronger than it was in 2008," says an analyst with \uc2 Kim Eng Securities\uc2 . Traders also expect a recovery in the IDR to support bargain buying later in the day. Meanwhile, Daiwa Capital Markets says in a note to investors that the correction in Indonesia's bank sector looks overdone, picking \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) as its top choice with a target of IDR8,850 vs Thursday's close of IDR5,200 (down 15%). (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds are higher due to increasing demand for safe-haven assets as the domestic equity market continues to fall, says a Shenzhen-based trader with a local insurer. "More and more investors are shifting their money to the fixed income market from stocks as they're pessimistic about the outlook for the world economy," he adds. The Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index is at 129.64 vs 129.62 at Thursday's close, and is tipped to test 129.70. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 22, 2011 22:20 ET (02:20 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110923e79n000l8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\