\par\par \b \uc2 South Korea Caps Week Of Fierce Won Defense\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jieun Shin and Min-Jeong Lee   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 711 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2011\par 13:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities fired a late salvo of dollar selling Friday to cap a week in which they dumped around $7.5 billion to $10 billion to support the beleaguered Korean won, some of its most aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the 2008 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The South Korean authorities have been among the most active in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets this week as central banks in emerging markets defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid a general flight from risky assets. Authorities in India, Taiwan and \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  were also seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday.\par \par \uc2 Traders say that the Bank of Korea, acting on behalf of Korea's finance ministry, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars each day this week, and on most days more than once, in an effort to stem a sharp slide in the won. On Friday alone, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold around $4 billion, dragging the dollar down near the end of the trading day from a high of 1,196 won to close at 1,166 won. The dollar has risen 4.8% this week and 9.3% since the start of September.\par \par \uc2 Since the authorities won't provide details of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the amounts involved can't be confirmed. Based on accounts from traders, a similar scale of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't been seen in the market since authorities defended the won during market turmoil in 2008.\par \par \uc2 Seoul's support for the won comes as South Korean markets have taken a hammering in recent weeks due to the continuing debt crisis in Europe and concerns about the health of the U.S. economy. South Korea's open, export-dependent economy means investors are often quick to exit Korean assets when there are worries about global growth. The main stock index closed down 5.7% Friday, the biggest single-day drop for a month, and is at its lowest level for over 14 months.\par \par \uc2 Local authorities have warned repeatedly in recent days that they are monitoring the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan said in an interview Thursday that the authorities are concerned about "excessive and unnecessary" volatility in the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market caused by capital flows.\par \par \uc2 Underscoring the level of concern, government officials met with executives at some of South Korea's biggest exporters on Friday, including \uc2 Samsung Electronics Co\uc2 . (005930.SE), to discuss the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The officials and companies wouldn't discuss details of the meeting, but analysts said the government likely asked the conglomerates to exchange their foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings for won to boost demand for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If the market knows there's real won demand [from exporters], they'll be more cautious about trading just in one direction," said Byeon Ji-young, an analyst at Woori Futures.\par \par \uc2 While a weaker won benefits the nation's big exporters, who account for around half of economic output, Korea is a heavy importer of basic commodities and won declines make those imports more expensive. Korea is currently grappling with inflation running well above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target levels.\par \par \uc2 Seoul has since mid-2010 taken some limited steps to stem flows of so-called hot money, including capping \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards positions of banks operating onshore to limit speculation in the won. Local authorities say these measures have damped some of the whipsawing in local markets but they concede there's only so much that can be done unilaterally to limit the effects of global economic turmoil on Korea's markets.\par \par \uc2 Speaking Thursday in Washington, Finance Minister Bahk called on the Group of 20 nations to come up with near-term steps to stabilize global markets. The G-20 said there would be an "action plan" of coordinated short- and medium-term economic policies to be announced at the G-20 summit in Cannes in early November.\par \par \uc2 For \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders in Seoul, the weekend brings a chance to recover from a hectic few days. "[I felt like I was] swept into a tsunami of [volatile] \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates," said Han Sung-min, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer based in Seoul.\par \par \uc2 --By Jieun Shin and Min-Jeong Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; jieun.shin@dowjones.com [ 09-23-11 0711ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110923e79n0002x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
