\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to prop up rupiah\b0\par\par\uc2 679 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2011\par 12:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Cbank buys nearly $200 mln debt, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  mkt\par \par \uc2 * Cbank says country's fundamentals strong\par \par \uc2 * State pension fund says sell-off a buying opportunity\par \par \uc2 (Updates with details, fund manager comment)\par \par \uc2 By Adriana Nina Kusuma and Neil Chatterjee\par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in on Thursday to prop up the rupiah, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and buying government bonds after selling by foreign investors drove the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sharply lower.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia bought 1.74 trillion rupiah ($196 million) of long-dated, mostly 21-year government bonds as it sought to stabilise a selloff that also took gains in benchmark 10-year sovereign yields to 120 basis points in two weeks.\par \par \uc2 "Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and buying government bonds in large amounts. The rupiah has returned to 8,750 per dollar," said Perry Warjiyo, Bank Indonesia's director for monetary policy and research.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah earlier on Thursday weakened as much as 2 percent to a low of 9,050 per dollar, before recovering following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One-month dollar/rupiah non-deliverable forwards\par \par \uc2 jumped to a near 2-year high as offshore players kept buying dollars amid insufficient liquidity in onshore markets, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 NDF demand from offshore investors such as real money accounts, along with the bond outflows, put pressure on the spot rupiah.\par \par \uc2 The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had risen to a seven-year high by the end of August as investors poured into Southeast Asia's biggest economy to take bond yields to record lows and stocks to record highs.\par \par \uc2 But as the outlook for global growth has soured, local policymakers have sounded increasingly nervous about a reversal in sentiment.\par \par \uc2 FOREIGN EXPOSURE\par \par \uc2 Memories of the 1997/98 Asian financial crisis are still fresh, when a sudden withdrawal of foreign funds and high levels of foreign debt rocked the region, prompting the IMF to step in to help stabilise Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia has made a series of moves this year to try to guard against a recurrence of such outflows that could destabilise its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and economy, pushing investors towards government bonds by curtailing its popular short-dated SBI notes.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month it said it will introduce a regulation requiring exporters to return export earnings parked overseas, in another effort to reduce dependence on foreign liquidity and boost the rupiah, but this will only take effect next year. It is also considering limiting firms' foreign debt exposure.\par \par \uc2 Despite these moves and the recovery in the rupiah, stocks slid 9 percent to wipe out this year's gains.\par \par \uc2 Policymakers are trying to prove the country is now more resilient to external shocks as they seek the approval of ratings agencies in the next year for an upgrade to an investment grade sovereign rating, which would lower government borrowing costs and spur fund investment interest.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has built up record foreign reserves, of $124.5 billion by mid-August, or over double the level in 2008, to give it plenty of ammunition to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Our fundamentals are strong -- high growth, low inflation, surplus balance of payments," said Warjiyo, who last week said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to loosen monetary policy to counter the impact of the global slowdown.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia is exposed to the risk of fund outflows as 35 percent of total tradeable bonds as of mid-August were owned by foreign investors, and foreign ownership in stocks is even higher.\par \par \uc2 The country's biggest pension fund Jamsostek said the sell-off, an "expression of anger" by investors over U.S and European economic woes, was a buying opportunity.\par \par \uc2 "Jamsostek is in a buying position at the moment given the cheap valuation of stocks in banking and resources. I'm buying bonds and stocks now," said Elvyn Masassya, chief investment officer at the state fund, which manages more than $13 billion.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 8870 Rupiah)\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Janeman Latul and Rieka Rahadiana; Editing by John Stonestreet)\par \par \uc2 INDONESIA-ECONOMY/BONDS  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110925e79m0008l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
