\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Emerging Markets Tumble World-Wide\b0\par\par\uc2 1,169 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2011\par 23:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   By\u8201\'20\'09 Tom Lauricella, Alex Frangos and \u8201\'20\'09John Lyons \par   Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Investors turned tail on emerging markets, sending their stocks, bonds and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  into steep declines, demonstrating that their relatively healthy economies are no match for the turmoil and uncertainty in the U.S. and Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Across Asia, most major stock markets fell 2% to 3% Thursday. Indonesian stocks plunged almost 9%. In Latin America, Brazilian and Mexican shares stocks dropped almost 5%.\par \par \uc2 While foreign investors have been steadily pulling money out of emerging-market stocks since July, the recent rout has been more aggressive. It has also been driven by investors who focus on bonds denominated in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than stocks.\par \par \uc2 The speed and scope of the declines serves as a reminder that emerging markets, while bigger and more active than in the past, can be still be treacherous and prone to sudden outflows of money, as happened during the peak of the financial crisis in 2008 and the Asian meltdown in 1998. As investors have turned away from riskier investments in general, emerging markets--where liquidity can quickly disappear--the result has been dramatic moves.\par \par \uc2 "These markets can be one-sided and they get one-sided fast," said Pablo Goldberg, head of global emerging-markets research at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, Brazil's real--until recently one of the world's biggest gainers against the U.S. dollar--slid nearly 6% against the dollar before clawing back much of its losses after Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deployed $2.7 billion to support it.\par \par \uc2 "We are ready, absolutely ready," to meet the crisis, Brazil's President Dilma Rousseff said in New York. "We are a country with very low debt, we have our accounts in order, and we are a country with high reserves."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was down 3% at 1.90 per dollar late in New York on Thursday. The real has now lost almost 9% in the past week and is down nearly 19% from its high against the dollar set in late July. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Mexico, Chile, Colombia and Peru also slid Thursday.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the bond market, buying its rupiah-denominated bonds to help support prices.\par \par \uc2 Despite the carnage, many investors remain optimistic about the medium- and longer-term prospects for many emerging market economies.\par \par \uc2 Unlike in their developed-market counterparts, many emerging-market central banks and governments are seen as having flexibility in their monetary and fiscal policy to soften the blow from a global economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's benchmark interest rate is one of the highest in the world at 12%, for example. Brazil cut the overnight lending rate by half a percentage point earlier this month, and more cuts are expected. Economists say central banks in other nations, such as Mexico, may also lower.\par \par \uc2 In addition, as was the case in 2008, the headwinds for emerging markets are coming from abroad. That, many investors believe, means that when markets calm, emerging markets should be in a better position to recover.\par \par \uc2 Emerging markets "are not the ones with the problems," said Sara Zervos, co-manager of the $14 billion Oppenheimer International Bond fund.\par \par \uc2 The International Monetary Fund, for example, is still predicting emerging market economies will grow by 6.1% in 2012, that's down just 0.3 percentage point from the June forecast. In contrast, the IMF lowered its forecasts for the world's developed economies by 0.7 percentage point to 1.9%.\par \par \uc2 Despite the longer-term optimism, emerging markets are being hit by a reversal of some of the huge amounts of investor money that had poured in over the past two years.\par \par \uc2 That is especially the case for bonds denominated in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . For much of the history of developing economy bond markets, their debt has been denominated in dollars because investors were wary of taking on the risk of owning notoriously volatile emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In recent years, however, as many emerging markets have successfully controlled inflation and improved their fiscal positions, their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have become less volatile and gained strength against the U.S. dollar. Investors looking to benefit from both rising values of emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and their generally higher interest rates have poured $54 billion into local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond funds, according to EPFR.\par \par \uc2 Even as emerging-market stocks faltered in August, investors moved $2.6 billion into local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond funds, after a $3.4 billion July inflow that was the third-biggest monthly gain for the funds on record, according to EPFR.\par \par \uc2 Because a prime attraction of local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds are the expected extra returns an investor can earn rising emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , most local bond investors left their positions unhedged against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations.\par \par \uc2 But with the turmoil in Europe leading investors to buy the dollar as a safe haven, local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond investors have begun to sell those bonds outright or hedge their exposure to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  using forward contracts.\par \par \uc2 Nicholas Chamie, global head of emerging markets research at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 , said the exodus from the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the timing of the selloff in emerging-market bonds, coming well after other riskier markets had started selling off in July, is typical.\par \par \uc2 "Emerging markets are less liquid and what you tend to see is the most liquid assets get sold first," he said. "Once the flow starts, the price action starts to gap and that's what we have started to see."\par \par \uc2 The yield on the benchmark Brazilian five-year note, for example, has jumped 0.75 percentage point to 12.20% from 11.45% since Sept. 12. That move over the past week and half reversed a rally that had taken place since mid-August.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's bond market as well has been the poster child for the flows into emerging-market debt, where investors have been attracted to higher yields and strong economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 That is now reversing. Indonesia government figures show foreigners have sold nearly $2 billion from the $78 billion Indonesian bond market in the past 10 days. Foreigners owned 15% of Indonesia's bonds in early 2010, rising to 36% in August. It now stands at 33%, indicating a major pullback could still await.\par \par \uc2 An exodus out of a relatively small and illiquid market like Indonesia can mean oversized price drops as everyone sells at the same time. The drop in the rupiah against the dollar is forcing stock investors to quickly liquidate or hedge their positions in other ways, such as shorting commodities or other stock markets that run in tandem with Indonesia's.\par \par \uc2 "It's a small tunnel to get through," said Robert Levitt, founder of Levitt Capital Management, which oversees $500 million in private client assets, with some of that invested in Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 -By\u8201\'20\'09 Tom Lauricella, The Wall Street Journal; 212-416-2768 [ 09-22-11 1717ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110922e79m00176\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
