\par\par \b \uc2 Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  Official Confirms  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  To Defend Rupiah\b0\par\par\uc2 316 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2011\par 15:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell to $122 billion as of Friday from $124.6 billion a week earlier due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the local unit and repayment of the government's offshore debt falling due during the period, deputy governor Hartadi Sarwono said Sunday.\par \par \uc2 "Negative sentiment over the global economy triggered by fears of worsening debt problems in Europe have triggered profit taking [on rupiah-related assets] by short-term investors," Sarwono told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 His comments confirmed speculation in the market that Bank Indonesia had sold dollars nearly every day last week as the local unit fell to its lowest level since March 14 with foreigners trimming their holdings in the local stocks, government bonds and Bank Indonesia money market securities.\par \par \uc2 Sarwono added that long-term "strategic investors, however, stay because of Indonesia's good prospects. We even detected some new strategic investors entered Indonesian markets."\par \par \uc2 The nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's deputy governor reaffirmed that Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary either by selling dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market or buying local government bonds using its rupiah or \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 He added that Bank Indonesia's action to defend the rupiah has restored investors' confidence and he believes the rupiah will resume its upward trend in the near future.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia has been relying on the rupiah's appreciation to help curb inflation, and has been avoiding rate hikes on fears higher rates would further attract short-term capital.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia had experienced robust short-term fund inflows in the past two years attracted by relatively higher yields offered by rupiah assets and its sound economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 -By Joko Hariyanto; contributing to Dow Jones Newswires: 62 21 3983 1277: i-made.sentana@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 18, 2011 09:11 ET (13:11 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110918e79i002i4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
