\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Extend drop in Asia, won hits 6-mth low\b0\par\par\uc2 1,091 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2011\par 06:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Won hits 6-mth low, ringgit 9-mth trough, peso 6-mth low\par \par \uc2 * Stops add to dlr/won rise, c.bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tempers gain\par \par \uc2 * Hedge funds, offshore players cited buying dollar/Asia\par \par \uc2 * Rupiah dips but stays above previous day's low\par \par \uc2 (Updates text, prices)\par \par \uc2 By Masayuki Kitano and Rick Lloyd\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 15 (Reuters) - The dollar rose broadly against emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, with the South Korean won hitting a six-month low on selling by offshore players and stop-loss offers.\par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah stabilised somewhat after selling off sharply the previous day, when offshore real money investors were selling Indonesian government bonds. The rupiah fell but stayed above a trough hit on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Pressure on emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  persisted overall, with the Malaysian ringgit hitting a nine-month trough and the Philippine peso dipping to its lowest in six months.\par \par \uc2 "At this point we do not rule out further potential weakness for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Emmanuel Ng, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at OCBC in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have slid sharply this week as heightened jitters over the euro zone's sovereign debt crisis and the impact on the European banking sector, stoked risk aversion among investors.\par \par \uc2 Dollar buying by hedge funds and position unwinding by offshore investors have pummelled Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which until recently, had remained relatively stable, stirring talk that they were increasingly being regarded as safe havens by investors.\par \par \uc2 "This was pretty much the last frontier, in terms of the... risk that was still on the table," said OCBC's Ng.\par \par \uc2 Hedge funds and offshore players were buying dollar again on Thursday, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Asian central banks, which spent an estimated $4 billion in dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, were spotted selling dollars again on Thursday, with traders citing dollar-selling by the central banks of South Korea and Indonesia as well as the Philippines.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Cavenagh, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for \uc2 Westpac\uc2  in Singapore, said dollar/Asia may start to see some consolidation in the near-term, but cautioned against jumping into short dollar/Asia positions at this point.\par \par \uc2 "The situation in Europe remains far from resolved and global growth is continuing to cool. The medium term outlook for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains upbeat but we suspect better entry levels for short USD/Asia positions will still present themselves over coming weeks," he said in a research note.\par \par \uc2 INDONESIAN RUPIAH\par \par \uc2 Dollar/rupiah stabilised somewhat after surging to its highest since late February of around 8,840 in the brokers' market on Wednesday. One trader said Thursday's high in dollar/rupiah was 8,800 .\par \par \uc2 Dollar-buying by offshore players was less active on Thursday than the previous day, traders said, adding that dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped temper the dollar's gains.\par \par \uc2 There was also talk of dollar-selling by leveraged names, while model funds were cited as dollar buyers.\par \par \uc2 Long-end Indonesian government bonds regained ground after the previous day's slide, with a dealer for a European bank in Singapore saying yields dropped roughly 15 to 20 basis points on the day .\par \par \uc2 The bond buying was led by onshore players, although there was some buying from offshore players as well, the dealer said.\par \par \uc2 KOREAN WON\par \par \uc2 Dollar selling against the won by onshore players initially weighed on the dollar, but momentum players and custodial names later bought dollar/won on dips.\par \par \uc2 Offshore players were among those cited as dollar buyers against the won, with traders saying offshore names were also spotted selling South Korean equities.\par \par \uc2 The dollar extended gains on stop-loss bids, hitting its highest in roughly six months of 1,119.9 , with an option barrier said to be lurking at 1,120.\par \par \uc2 Dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped temper the dollar's gains against the won, traders said.\par \par \uc2 RINGGIT\par \par \uc2 Dollar/ringgit hit a fresh nine-month high of 3.1030\par \par \uc2 . Fast money and model accounts were cited as pushing dollar/ringgit higher. In addition, a Kuala Lumpur-based trader said onshore and offshore players were spotted selling Malaysian government securities and buying dollar/ringgit.\par \par \uc2 PHILIPPINE PESO\par \par \uc2 Dollar/peso hit a six-month high of 43.700 . It was supported by USD short-covering by real money investors, traders said, adding that the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the dollar's rise.\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 Hedge fund manager William Ackman, best known for selecting stocks, said he was buying the Hong Kong dollar, noting that it could become his biggest bet ever.\par \par \uc2 The manager of New York-based \uc2 Pershing Square Capital Management\uc2 , said he believes that the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  will eventually allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate dramatically against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on the day at   0715 GMT                      \par                                                     \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct \par Move\par  Japan yen        76.69          76.64       \par -0.06\par  Sing dlr        1.2470         1.2451       \par -0.15\par  Taiwan dlr      29.593         29.625       \par +0.11\par  Korean won     1114.75        1107.80       \par -0.62\par  Baht             30.29          30.25       \par -0.13\par  Peso             43.35          43.22       \par -0.29\par  Rupiah         8740.00        8715.00       \par -0.29\par  Rupee            47.81          47.64       \par -0.35\par  Ringgit         3.0975         3.0787       \par -0.61\par  Yuan            6.3887         6.3964       \par +0.12\par  Change so far in 2001                                \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct \par Move\par  Japan yen        76.69          81.15       \par +5.82\par  Sing dlr        1.2470         1.2820       \par +2.81\par  Taiwan dlr      29.593         30.368       \par +2.62\par  Korean won     1114.75        1134.80       \par +1.80\par  Baht             30.29          30.14       \par -0.50\par  Peso             43.35          43.84       \par +1.14\par  Rupiah         8740.00        9005.00       \par +3.03\par  Rupee            47.81          44.70       \par -6.50\par  Ringgit         3.0975         3.0820       \par -0.50\par  Yuan