\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Indonesia Deputy Gov: Rupiah's Fall 'Playing Catch Up' With Regional  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 317 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2011\par 08:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not concerned about the rupiah's steep decline against the dollar Wednesday, Deputy Governor Hartadi Sarwono said, describing the decline as "playing catch up" with recent losses in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region.\par \par \uc2 "The average of the rupiah's weakness in the past five days is comparable to that of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 '... The rupiah's previous declines were not as deep as the others," Sarwono told Dow Jones Newswires on the sidelines of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  event, suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not concerned about the drop.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah lost ground against the greenback Wednesday, with the dollar reaching an intraday peak of IDR8,820, the highest since March 14 and compared with IDR8,660 late Tuesday, as global risk aversion hit the country's assets. Yield on 10-year rupiah-denominated government bonds climbed to around 7.2% from 6.7% Tuesday, while the main stock index was down 1.8% at midday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar trimmed some of its gains and was quoted IDR8,730 at 0620 GMT, with traders saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely sold dollars to stem the its rise.\par \par \uc2 "The most important thing is to ensure the market is not panicking...For today, it is unlikely for us to buy back government bonds as we have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the (spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) market," Sarwono added.\par \par \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month said it will buy back rupiah-denominated government bonds from investors, using its dollar reserves, if it is approached. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will sell dollars and use the rupiah proceeds to fund the buyback, a move that could support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond market in the event of a massive sell-off.\par \par \uc2 -By I Made Sentana, Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 39831277; i-made.sentana@dowjones.com [ 14-09-11 0639GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110914e79e000f9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
