\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(8/24) Emerging Nations Seek To Bolster  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2011\par 02:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par C5\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL) \par    By Erin McCarthy \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some emerging nations have stepped into the market this month to prevent steeper declines in their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 The darkening economic outlook has turned the status quo for emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  upside down. In recent weeks Turkey, South Korea and Indonesia have taken or forewarned of measures to curb their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' slides after global growth sent investors fleeing riskier assets, including equities and emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 These central banks and others want to prevent their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from swinging sharply in either direction -- too steep a depreciation could scare off long-term foreign investment, while a too-strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hurts exports. But a global slowdown could simplify the bankers' task, as emerging nations may need to put in fewer controls to head off strength in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  if investment flows moderate.\par \par \uc2 "Most emerging markets still need external financing, and if they felt like that wall of money is suddenly withdrawing, they might loosen up in terms of their controls," said Sara Zervos, a senior portfolio manager at Oppenheimer Funds.\par \par \uc2 South Korean authorities earlier this month said they would step up monitoring of local financial markets, including conditions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, amid heightened volatility. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers suspected Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of selling dollars to support the won amid a plunge in the local stock market in early August. The won has weakened more than 2% this month against the dollar, though it still is up for the year.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut rates and bought dollars earlier this year in order to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In recent weeks, the bank has canceled U.S. dollar-buying auctions and instead began auctions to sell dollars. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its benchmark rate on hold Tuesday. The lira has fallen nearly 5% since Aug. 3 and is down about 15% this year.\par \par \uc2 Some Asian central banks, including Indonesia's, also have been playing a balancing act, though on a more subdued scale. This month, Bank Indonesia said it will buy back rupiah-denominated government bonds from investors if it is approached and said it will sell dollars and use the rupiah proceeds to fund the buybacks. Dealers also suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market by selling dollars earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the trend is still toward stronger emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as long as developing economies see robust growth. They add that any efforts by emerging-market central banks to fight depreciation are likely to be short-term, and more stable markets may erase the need to do so at all.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 23, 2011 20:09 ET (00:09 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110824e78o00063\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
