\par\par \b \uc2 DJ CORRECT: MARKET TALK: Spain Resists Bond Market Contagion\b0\par\par\uc2 1,457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2011\par 12:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ("MARKET TALK: Spain Resists Bond Market Contagion - Trader," at 1029 GMT, misstated the day. The correct version follows:)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1029 GMT [Dow Jones] Some investors are buying Spanish and Italian bonds Thursday, ignoring the fresh bout of weakness in Portugal, Greece, and Ireland, a trader says. "Some accounts are looking at the 'good' peripherals. People believe the Spain recovery story and the post-auction performance has been great." Spain's 5.5% April 2021 bond, auctioned Wednesday at an average yield of 5.472%, yields 5.451% Thursday, according to Tradeweb.\par \par \uc2 (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1035 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian rupiah-denominated government bonds are mixed ahead of the extended weekend as some players take profit after Wednesday's gains. Still, traders say foreign players continue to enter the market, helping to lift the IDR to its highest level against the USD in seven years. "With the rupiah expected to rise further, on top of over 6.0% GDP growth this year, our bonds are very attractive to foreigners," says a local trader. The five-year yield is higher at 6.92% from 6.87% from late Wednesday, the 10-year yield is up at 7.76% from 7.73%, the 15-year yield is flat at 8.36% and the 20-year is lower at 9.01% from 9.04%. The local market will be closed for a holiday Friday. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1029 GMT [Dow Jones] Some investors are buying Spanish and Italian bonds Friday, ignoring the fresh bout of weakness in Portugal, Greece, and Ireland, a trader says. "Some accounts are looking at the 'good' peripherals. People believe the Spain recovery story and the post-auction performance has been great." Spain's 5.5% April 2021 bond, auctioned Wednesday at an average yield of 5.472%, yields 5.451% Thursday, according to Tradeweb. (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1021 GMT [Dow Jones] Russian outflows in the week to April 15 amounted to $1.1B, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says, analyzing weekly reserve data. "We link the capital outflows with the weak investment data since the start of the year, which could be a result of perceived political risks. As the greater clarity on 2012 presidential election emerges, we expect capital flows to recover further into the second quarter." (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1016 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  changes its stance on spreads between Italian government bonds and bunds to neutral from a previous bet spreads would narrow. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  still expects Italian BTPs to outperform other peripheral euro-zone sovereigns. It says Italy's government is likely to beat its 2011 budget deficit target of 3.9%, but \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  warns that fiscal consolidation from 2012 on will get tougher if economic growth remains slow. And it suggests more pension reform is needed, noting benefits in Italy are amongst the highest in Europe and their duration the longest. Italian 10Y BTPs yielded 4.72% a short while ago, up 4 basis points, vs German bunds flat at 3.32% and Spanish 10Y yields at 5.44% down 2 bp. (chris.emsden@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1012 GMT [Dow Jones] Inflationary pressure in Hong Kong is likely to rise further in coming months, economists say. Hong Kong's March composite CPI rose 4.6% on-year, marking the fastest pace in over two-and-half years, boosted by higher private rental costs and food prices, the Census and Statistics Department says Thursday. The increase was higher than February's 3.7% rise, and the median 4.1% rise forecast by 11 economists surveyed by Dow Jones. Hang Seng Bank Senior Economist Irina Fan says major drivers for inflation--the surge in global food prices and strong internal demand--are unlikely to be eased in the near term, and "imported inflation is weighing in, especially when other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are rising and the Hong Kong dollar is unchanged and pegged with the U.S. dollar." (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1012 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  cuts its 1Q UK GDP forecast to +0.5% on the quarter from +0.7% in the light of subdued retail sales data in 1Q and more "general anecdotal evidence of weak consumer demand." The bank, however, doesn't change its growth profile beyond 1Q, and forecasts GDP growth of 1.7% in 2011 versus 1.8% previously. Inflation levels are expected to remain elevated, with CPI and RPI peaking in September at 5.0% and 6.4% respectively. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1003 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR falls further, and is currently at 8,623, the lowest level in seven years, from 8,645 late Wednesday on capital inflows to the local stock and bond markets, traders say. "I think Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the pair goes lower, to avoid more volatility. Immediate support level is likely at 8,615," says a local trader. He tips the pair in a 8,615-8,635 range next week. The local market will be closed for a public holiday Friday. Indonesian shares end up 0.2% at a record high of 3801.081.(andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1003 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds are trading in a narrow range after yield cutoffs were broadly in line, says a trader at a state-run bank. The most-active 8.08% 2022 bond is at 99.77 from 99.81 before the cutoff data, while the benchmark 7.80% 2021 bond is at 99.35 from 99.40. "The cutoffs are mostly in line, but one fear is the issuance of more cash management bills by the government, which will keep prices under pressure," the trader says. He adds, there could be some recovery if cash management bills aren't issued as there are no scheduled bond auctions next week. He tips the 8.08% 2022 bond yield in a 8.22%-8.28% near-term band vs 8.25% currently. The cutoff for the 7.83% 2018 bond yield is at 8.09% compared with the 8.10% forecast in a Dow Jones poll, while the yield cut-off for the 7.80% 2021 bond is 8.06% vs the 8.08% forecast, and the 8.30% 2040 bond yield cutoff is at 8.499% vs the 8.525% forecast. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1000 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says it will likely re-enter the Hungarian market after Easter holidays following the government's announcement of stronger-than-expected fiscal cuts for 2012. Economy Minister Gyorgy Matolcsy says the government plans to reduce spending on public-sector wages and remove personal income tax benefits in coming years. \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  expects these steps on top of the already-announced fiscal package worth around 3% of GDP; sees the additional amount around 1% of GDP in 2012. Says the government announcement is good news, suggesting Hungary is determined to meet its fiscal goals. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1000 GMT [Dow Jones] CZK trades slightly weaker at 24.175-24.225 against the euro from 24.139 late Wednesday as the koruna lacks momentum amid a mixed equity market and investors closing positions ahead of the long weekend, a trader says. "The only other thing interesting for the koruna was [\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] vice-governor Hampl's comments yesterday, which were even a bit dovish as regards the short term, so EUR/CZK may move a bit higher towards 24.25 today and tomorrow," the trader says. (sean.carney@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 5:56 (Dow Jones) The quarter-point increase in Brazil's benchmark interest rate doesn't necessary signal the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sees inflation pressures easing, says M Safra economist Marcelo Fonseca. Instead, it just means the bank is sticking to its strategy of using "gradual increases" over a longer period to fight inflation, "which in my view hasn't produced the desired fruits." But \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  says the move means the government will have to be "more aggressive" in future macroprudential measures and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will need to increase at least a quarter-point at its next meeting in June. BBH contends the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is falling behind the curve and its "unorthodox" measures aren't working, leaving doubt as to when inflation will decline after it likely breaks past Brazil's inflation-target limit of 6.5% this quarter. (paulo.winterstein@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2354 or email kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Visit the Market Talk blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 21, 2011 06:43 ET (10:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110421e74l002hs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
