\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Indonesia holds rates for now, using rupiah vs inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 By Aditya Suharmoko   \par \uc2 523 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2011\par 10:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, quotes from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and economists)\par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, April 12 (Reuters) - Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its benchmark interest rate at 6.75 percent on Tuesday, as expected, and said it was letting the rupiah rise as part of its efforts to contain inflationary pressures caused by strong domestic demand and high commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 Economists said those worries about inflation meant interest rates were bound to go higher but Bank Indonesia decided to hold policy on this occasion because inflation dipped in March and the current harvest would help take pressure off food prices.\par \par \uc2 "In our view, it is wise to probably tighten further, given the economy is still very strong. We saw some ministers said 6.5 percent growth for Q1 -- that's a very strong number," said Wellian Wiranto, an economist with \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "We still think they will hike in second quarter, even as soon as next month. (Our forecast) is they will rise 25 basis points next month and another 25 basis points in June, so a total of 50 basis points in Q2."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects economic growth to remain solid in the second quarter, with Governor Darmin Nasution telling a news conference after a policy meeting that he expected an expansion of 6.4 percent from the same quarter last year.\par \par \uc2 Annual inflation slowed to 6.65 percent in March from 6.84 percent in February, although core inflation, which excludes administered prices and volatile food, edged up to 4.45 percent from 4.36 percent.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah has strengthened nearly 4 percent against the dollar in 2011. At mid-afternoon Tuesday, it was at around 8,655 to the dollar. In the past week, it has touched its strongest level since May 2004.\par \par \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to put brakes on the rupiah's appreciation, but not aggressively.\par \par \uc2 "Like other regional central banks, Bank Indonesia recognises the benefit of an appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in helping to contain inflation pressures," said David Cohen at Action Economics in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Any worries about loss of competitiveness from the rise in the rupiah were mitigated by the rise in other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Nasution said competitiveness had not been hurt.\par \par \uc2 However, to slow the influx of hot money coming into the country, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced that from May 13 investors would have to hold on to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt (SBI) for six months before selling it, against one month now.\par \par \uc2 Analysts forecast Bank Indonesia will raise rates to 7 percent by the end of this quarter and to 7.5 percent by the year-end to dampen inflation in Southeast Asia's largest economy.\par \par \uc2 Rising oil prices may force the government to implement a plan to cut fuel subsidies for private cars, which has been delayed, or increase the retail price of subsidised fuel sometime in the second quarter, which will add to inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Aditya Suharmoko and Adriana Nina Kusuma; Writing by Alan Raybould)\par \par \uc2 INDONESIA-ECONOMY/RATE (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110412e74c000d0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*