\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Surge With Euro, Prompting  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,133 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2011\par 14:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  surge as euro climbs on ECB, dollar slumps on budget\par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  spotted in Philippines, Malaysia, Korea, \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Thailand, India, Taiwan\par \par \uc2 -- Region's central banks buy $7.5 billion, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  estimates\par \par \uc2 -- Singapore dollar logs record high, Australian dollar highest since 1983 float, Korean won 2.5-year peak\par \par \uc2 -- No letup seen for dollar weakness until the Fed ends QE2 in June\par \par \uc2 (Adds estimated total for region, Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments, Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By P.R. Venkat \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  surged Friday as the euro climbed on an interest-rate hike and the dollar languished on a potential U.S. government shutdown, prompting several central banks in the region to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ' gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the sharp gainers, the Singapore dollar broke from its recent stagnancy to pop to a record high against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Australian dollar again notched its highest level since being floated in 1983 and the South Korean won hit a two-and-a-half-year high.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central banks in the Philippines, Malaysia, Korea, Indonesia, Thailand, India and Taiwan apparently bought dollars to keep the advance in their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  under control, local traders said. All told, the region's central banks bought $7.5 billion, Ron Ng of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  Global estimated in a research note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders began the day expecting the dollar might rise against Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as investors looked set to take profits on recent dollar declines to square their positions heading into the weekend.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Instead, the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remained weak as the euro added to gains from Thursday's rate hike by the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , its first tightening since 2008. Many Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  take their cue from the euro, which has acted as a barometer of global risk appetite.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell against most major and regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as hopes faded that Republicans and Democrats in Washington could agree on a budget deal before the U.S. government runs out of operating cash at midnight Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The tightening cycle in Europe has clearly started, and the rate-differential argument is pertinent here," while U.S. budget worries weighed on the dollar, said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Nizam Idris at \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Singapore.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian central banks may try to cushion the dollar's fall against their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  but know they can't stop the trend, and are simply trying to keep their export-reliant economies roughly competitive with their neighbors'. The authorities seldom comment on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but officials at several central banks in recent days have said they will act to prevent excessive volatility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, Bank Indonesia Deputy Gov. Budi Mulia told Dow Jones Newswires on Friday the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue to fight inflation, suggesting tolerance of rupiah gains, while preventing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's moves from being too volatile.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The region's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have surged over the past year as Asia has led the global economic recovery and as low interest rates in the richest countries have spurred a flood of investor capital into Asia seeking higher returns.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also got a boost as the People's Bank of China fixed the tightly controlled yuan higher Friday for a sixth session, setting the dollar's central parity rate at a record low CNY6.5420.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Expectations that Beijing will allow faster yuan gains sent the yuan sharply higher in the market for nondeliverable forwards, an offshore venue where investors can bet on future moves. The 12-month dollar/yuan contract sank to a three-year low bid quote of CNY6.3620, from CNY6.4000.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The region's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities are generally tolerating greater appreciation these days to help them battle rising inflation, said Thomas Harr, head of Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at Standard Chartered Bank in Singapore. The dollar will likely remain weak until at least June, when the Federal Reserve is slated to end its "quantitative easing" policy of buying government bonds, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday's euro ascent kick-started the Asia rally. The common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had come off Thursday's highs as the ECB's quarter-percentage-point rate hike was almost universally expected and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief Jean-Claude Trichet said the ECB hadn't decided to embark on a series of rate increases.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nonetheless, the euro took off in the Asia morning on the view that the ECB may tighten every quarter, and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  never looked back.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro's rise to a 2011 high above $1.44 spurred accelerating gains among Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , notably the Singapore dollar. The U.S. dollar had been largely holding above support around S$1.26 for the past two weeks, but the euro's climb helped break through that level. At 0945 GMT, the euro was at $1.4399, and the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought S$1.2571.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Whoever is long dollar will be suffering here," said Idris.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, a broad measure of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's value against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of America's main trading partners, was at 75.185 late in the Asian session. That was down from 75.534 late in New York and threatened a slide to its November 2009 low of 74.17 in coming weeks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To tame the peso at five-month highs, the Philippine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  apparently bought $600 million between PHP43.05 and PHP43.00 in heavy trade, defending the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  around the psychologically important PHP43.00 line, Manila traders said. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  apparently twice sold the ringgit, which has benefited as a proxy for the rising Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Kuala Lumpur traders said. They spotted the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buying dollars at MYR3.0210 late in the day after a defense at MYR3.0220 failed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea apparently bought an estimated $1 billion as the dollar fell to 1,082.00, its lowest since August 2008, when the global financial crisis was escalating, Seoul dealers said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a bid to curb the rising rupiah and baht, Bank Indonesia apparently bought an estimated $100 million at IDR8,653, while the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  apparently bought dollars around THB30.03, local dealers said. After the rupee hit a six-month high, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  was seen apparently buying dollars at INR43.98, the first \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since November, local traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Taiwan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  let the local dollar strengthen more than local traders had expected, but they spotted the bank buoying the greenback late in the day at TWD28.903.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By P.R. Venkat, Dow Jones Newswires; +65 64154 152; venkat.pr@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cris Larano and Rhea Sandique-Carlos in Manila, Min-Jeong Lee in Seoul and Anant Vijay Kala in New Delhi contributed to this article.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 08, 2011 08:57 ET (12:57 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110408e748002u8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picsca