\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Indonesia: To Keep Inflation Under Control, Prevent Rupiah Volatility\b0\par\par\uc2 216 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2011\par 08:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Bank Indonesia will continue to fight inflation and prevent the rupiah from being overly volatile, Deputy Gov. Budi Mulia said Friday.\par \par \uc2 Mulia's statement could indicate that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains open to the option of raising interest rates if inflation picks up again, and allowing the rupiah to continue rising to help rein in imported inflation, although it is likely to want the rupiah's appreciation against the dollar to be smooth.\par \par \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market often in recent weeks to buy dollars to curb the rupiah's sharp rise as capital inflows remain strong.\par \par \uc2 Mulia told Dow Jones Newswires that Bank Indonesia will prevent "excessive risk-taking."\par \par \uc2 He added that the upgrade of Indonesia's ratings by \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  Ratings Services Friday to BB+ from BB shows that the appreciation in the local unit has been supported by economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 Separately, Bank Indonesia Gov. Darmin Nasution told reporters in Bali that the ratings upgrade is expected to attract more long-term fund inflows.\par \par \uc2 -By I Made Sentana, Dow Jones Newswires; +62 21-39831277; I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Andreas Ismar contributed to this article [ 08-04-11 0635GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110408e748000d5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
