\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Oil Traders Should Also Watch USD - GS Caltex\b0\par\par\uc2 1,699 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2011\par 07:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0550 GMT [Dow Jones] Supply problems in Libya and other oil-exporting states remain the dominant driver of crude's rally, with concerns over election related potential production disruptions in Nigeria supporting benchmark contracts in Asian trade Friday. Nigeria's electoral agency on Thursday announced a third delay in legislative polls in some parts of the country. Traders say any kind of supply problems in Africa's top oil producing nation will likely squeeze an already tight global oil market and trigger a long-term bull signal for crude. Nymex crude for May delivery is up 85 cents at $111.15/bbl after hitting a fresh 2.5-year high of $111.19 earlier in the session. In addition, oil traders should also keep an eye on the USD, says a GS Caltex trader. The USD is weaker against the EUR in Asian trade after the ECB delivered its first rate hike since 2008 Thursday; the greenback's weakness will likely provide extra support for dollar-denominated crude prices. "There are just too many factors pushing oil prices up," says the trader. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0545 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end 0.1% lower at 8894,54, ending a six-session winning streak, dragged by profit-taking in tech heavyweights HTC (2498.TW) and Hon Hai (2317.TW), although hotel operators soar on news Taiwan will allow Chinese individual tourists to visit by end-June. Diana Wu, an analyst at Capital Securities, tips the Taiex to remain in a 8750-9000 range next week as fund rotation will remain the trading theme, with banking stocks likely outperforming as local interest rates are steadily rising. HTC ends 3.3% lower at NT$1,160.00 and Hon Hai sheds 1.8% to NT$107.50, while Ambassador Hotel (2704.TW) rises 3.3% to NT$44.40. Among other gainers, Compal (2324.TW) is +4.6% at NT$29.80 on its share buyback and \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) is up 1.6% at NT$26.00 on improved average selling prices in March. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0539 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 ends down 0.2% to 3445.28 after trading in a tight range; Hamilton Hindin Greene broker James Smalley notes that trading was fairly mixed with large cap Fletcher Building adding 0.2% to NZ$9.37. Earlier Friday the company said its revenue and earnings before interest and tax will increase following its takeover of Australian firm Crane Group (CRG.AU). Fletcher's market capitalization is now around NZ$6.2 billion compared with NZ$5.1 billion prior to its purchase of Crane. \uc2 Ryman Healthcare\uc2  (RYM.NZ) added 2.5% to NZ$2.48, bolstered by news demand for its retirement villages remains strong. In the other direction Telecom (TEL.NZ) shed 1.0% to NZ$1.985. Looking ahead, Smalley said the market would continue to focus on offshore markets for direction. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0536 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are down 0.2% at 1087.37, but off the intraday low of 1081.64, at the midday break, as losses due to profit-taking are trimmed by bargain hunting; trade is active. "It's normal to see some profit-taking after a sharp rally. However, it doesn't change a thing. The momentum is still biased upward," says KT Zmico Securities' First Vice President Charoen Iamphattanatham. He tips a 1080-1100 range. Among the most active stocks, IRPC (IRPC.TH) is down 1.6% at THB6.35, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) is down 1.3% at THB115.50, \uc2 Indorama Ventures\uc2  (IVL.TH) is down 0.5% at THB54.75, PTT (PTT.TH) is up 0.5% at THB379 and PTT Aromatics & Refining (PTTAR.TH) is up 1.8% at THB42.50. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0536 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR/CHF is likely to appreciate to 1.35 in three months and to 1.40 in six months as financial markets are proving resilient to risk, ING says. "We look for the funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the U.S. dollar, the yen and the Swiss franc to underperform this year, while the euro and emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should shine," says strategist Chris Turner. The EUR/CHF is at 1.3111. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0531 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB slips to 30.03-30.04 from 30.06-30.07 early, and compared with 30.11-30.115 late Thursday in active trade, in line with most regional peers, say traders. The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  is suspected to have bought the dollar since early trade, say two local dealers; they expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be around for the entire day to slow the baht's rise. One local dealer adds that continued capital inflows, in addition to exporters' selling, are pushing the greenback lower. Another dealer says the rising interest rate trend amid robust economic growth in Asia is likely to continue to attract more fund inflows. Dealers tip support at 30.00 for today. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] India's overnight call rate is likely to remain below 6.0% for the next two weeks on a positive banking system cash situation and as the RBI extends the second liquidity facility until March 6, says a dealer with a privately-run bank. The measures were set to expire Friday. The RBI says banks can avail liquidity up to 1.0% of their net deposits and can apply for a waiver if they fall short of the mandated bond-holding ratio due to this measure. "This will flush the banking system with liquidity in the short-term but may not be sustainable in the long-run," the dealer adds. The interbank overnight call rate is at 5.85% unchanged from Thursday's close, while the three-day call rate is at 6.0%. The collateralized borrowing and lending obligation rate or CBLO is at 2.16% from 3.25% at the open. The dealer tips the overnight call rate in a 5.00%-6.00% range in the near-term.(sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0523 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P's upgrade of Indonesia sovereign ratings raises expectations the country could get the long-coveted investment grade by the three major ratings agency by 2012, says Standard Chartered economist Eric Sugandi. "One of them could upgrade in 2011," he says. S&P raised its long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sovereign credit and debt ratings to BB+ with a positive outlook from BB. Its ratings are now at par with Fitch's BB+ (positive), and \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Ba1 (stable outlook). Analysts don't expect S&P's action to push bond prices much higher after their recent rally. "Bond yields have been very low," Danamon bond analyst Helmi Arman says. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0520 GMT (Dow Jones) The near-term risk for USD/CHF has reverted to the downside, and a retracement into the 0.9025 area is possible, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . "Intraday rallies are likely to find near term resistance at 0.9225 ahead of 0.9300," according to analyst Karen Anne Jones. The USD/CHF is at 0.9101 by 0515 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0512 GMT [Dow Jones] An investor sells with-delta one-month USD-call/JPY-put contract with a Y86.00 strike at 10.65% earlier in the day, while some other players sell three-month at-the-money straddles, says a senior dealer at a major Japanese bank. Traded contracts suggest "investors believe the USD/JPY will eventually rise above Y86.00, but not at a speed that the current volatilities suggest now," he says, adding that current USD/JPY volatilities are bit overpriced due to the Japan-quake risk premium. Benchmark volatilities implied by one-month ATM volatilities rise to 10.05%/10.75% from 10.25%/10.55% in New York; the dealer says ongoing concerns about Fukushima nuclear reactors are a part of the reason.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0507 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore STI is up marginally, gaining 2.89 points, or 0.1%, to 3174.54 at lunch after opening weaker as markets in Japan recover smartly from a lackluster start after Thursday's earthquake. "We may see some profit-taking again moving into the weekend. The markets are likely to remain cautious. A lot will depend on how the European markets react to the ECB rate hike," says OCBC analyst Carey Wong. Support for the benchmark is tipped below 3165 and then 3130 and resistance at 3180 followed by 3200. SGX (S68.SG) is among the top gainers, +2.7% at S$8.37 as Australia formally rejects merger plans with ASX (ASX.AU). \uc2 Sembcorp Industries\uc2  (U96.SG) is the top gainer, up 3.0% at S$5.51 and its rig building unit \uc2 Sembcorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG) is up 1.2% at S$6.00 on a spurt in crude oil prices. \uc2 Keppel Corp\uc2 . (BN4.SG) is up 0.2% at S$12.70. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0505 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR slides further to 8,653 vs 8,660 earlier in the day and 8,672 late yesterday as news of Indonesia's long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sovereign credit and debt ratings upgrade by S&P to BB+ from BB fuels more inflows, dealers say. "Although S&P was behind other ratings agencies its ratings action on Indonesia, it's still good news for Indonesia as now all three major ratings agencies have acknowledged Indonesia's progress," a dealer says; three dealers suspect Bank Indonesia to have bought around $100 million at the 8,653 level to ease volatility. "Despite Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the market still looks intent to drive the dollar lower," one of the dealers say. A 8,645-8,660 range is tipped. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com) [ 04-08-11 0150ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110408e7480003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
