\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/CHF May Hit 1.40 In 6 Mos On Risk Resilience-ING\b0\par\par\uc2 1,699 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2011\par 07:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0536 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR/CHF is likely to appreciate to 1.35 in three months and to 1.40 in six months as financial markets are proving resilient to risk, ING says. "We look for the funding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the U.S. dollar, the yen and the Swiss franc to underperform this year, while the euro and emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should shine," says strategist Chris Turner. The EUR/CHF is at 1.3111. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0531 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB slips to 30.03-30.04 from 30.06-30.07 early, and compared with 30.11-30.115 late Thursday in active trade, in line with most regional peers, say traders. The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  is suspected to have bought the dollar since early trade, say two local dealers; they expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be around for the entire day to slow the baht's rise. One local dealer adds that continued capital inflows, in addition to exporters' selling, are pushing the greenback lower. Another dealer says the rising interest rate trend amid robust economic growth in Asia is likely to continue to attract more fund inflows. Dealers tip support at 30.00 for today. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] India's overnight call rate is likely to remain below 6.0% for the next two weeks on a positive banking system cash situation and as the RBI extends the second liquidity facility until March 6, says a dealer with a privately-run bank. The measures were set to expire Friday. The RBI says banks can avail liquidity up to 1.0% of their net deposits and can apply for a waiver if they fall short of the mandated bond-holding ratio due to this measure. "This will flush the banking system with liquidity in the short-term but may not be sustainable in the long-run," the dealer adds. The interbank overnight call rate is at 5.85% unchanged from Thursday's close, while the three-day call rate is at 6.0%. The collateralized borrowing and lending obligation rate or CBLO is at 2.16% from 3.25% at the open. The dealer tips the overnight call rate in a 5.00%-6.00% range in the near-term.(sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0523 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P's upgrade of Indonesia sovereign ratings raises expectations the country could get the long-coveted investment grade by the three major ratings agency by 2012, says Standard Chartered economist Eric Sugandi. "One of them could upgrade in 2011," he says. S&P raised its long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sovereign credit and debt ratings to BB+ with a positive outlook from BB. Its ratings are now at par with Fitch's BB+ (positive), and \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Ba1 (stable outlook). Analysts don't expect S&P's action to push bond prices much higher after their recent rally. "Bond yields have been very low," Danamon bond analyst Helmi Arman says. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0520 GMT (Dow Jones) The near-term risk for USD/CHF has reverted to the downside, and a retracement into the 0.9025 area is possible, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . "Intraday rallies are likely to find near term resistance at 0.9225 ahead of 0.9300," according to analyst Karen Anne Jones. The USD/CHF is at 0.9101 by 0515 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0512 GMT [Dow Jones] An investor sells with-delta one-month USD-call/JPY-put contract with a Y86.00 strike at 10.65% earlier in the day, while some other players sell three-month at-the-money str