\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Indonesian Shares End Off 0.4%; Tipped To Fall Wed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,521 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2011\par 11:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0943 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares end slightly lower on profit-taking, and are expected to continue sliding Wednesday as most stocks are already overbought, dealers say. The main index ends down 0.4% at 3685.94, with 122 decliners and 88 gainers. Foreign net selling is estimated at a meager IDR100 billion, dealers add. "I expect the market to slowly move downward tomorrow," says Benny Irawan, fund manager at Lautandhana Sekuritas; he tips a 3640-3700 range for the main index for the rest of the week. Bumi (BUMI.JK) is down 2.3% at IDR3,175, Telkom (TLKM.JK) falls 0.7% to IDR7,250 and \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) sheds 2.9% to IDR6,650; but coal mining contractor \uc2 United Tractors\uc2  is up 1.6% at IDR22,400 on optimism over its outlook as it plans to raise IDR6 trillion via rights issue to finance expansion; its parent \uc2 Astra International\uc2  (ASII.JK) is up 0.4% at IDR56,650. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0939 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds are mixed, with medium-term notes pushing slightly higher; trade is light as investors take to the sidelines ahead of a Thai national holiday Wednesday, says a Bangkok-based dealer. "This morning, we saw a continuation of buying flows from offshore names, although in the afternoon they were selling, but only a little. Local banks have also still been on the buying side," he says. The market direction on Thursday will likely hinge on any influencing news or data during the holiday, though capital inflows into Thailand aren't expected to slow soon. The bid/offer yield of bonds due December 2015 are quoted at 3.26/3.23% from 3.26%/3.24% late Monday, the October 2017 yield is at 3.45%/3.42% from 3.47%/3.45% and the December 2021 yield is at 3.68%/3.64% from 3.68%/3.66%. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0922 GMT [Dow Jones] India's small and mid cap indexes are outperforming the Sensex as investors view the benchmark is likely to consolidate around the current level until the corporate results season is underway, says a local broker. "Investors are rebalancing their portfolios and there are quite a few good value stocks among the mid cap and small cap shares that have to catch up with the larger performers in their respective sectors," says the broker; he adds, "we are going to witness this trend on and off for the next few weeks." The Sensex is down 0.3% at 19,639.42 points, off its intraday high of 19,770.21; the Mid Cap index is +0.8%, buoyed by \uc2 Indiabulls Real Estate\uc2 's (532832.BY) 2.3% rise to INR136.05 and Voltas' (500575.BY) 2.4% gain to INR184.50. The Small Cap index is +1.2%, buoyed by \uc2 VIP Industries\uc2  (507880.BY) 1.1% rise to INR702.70 and \uc2 Dhanlaxmi Bank\uc2 's (532180.BY) 2.0% gain to INR126.30. (raghavendra.upadhyaya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0918 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is at a fresh low since early May 2007 at 8,655 in late trade vs 8,665 late yesterday as the government's IDR7.0 trillion bond auction attracts fund inflows, dealers say. "I didn't see Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market today, but it may buy the dollar tomorrow if it continues to slide," a dealer says; Bank Indonesia has been frequently suspected to have bought the greenback recently to slow its slide. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to be tolerating some downside for the pair to help curtail imported inflation. Dealers tip a 8,640-8,670 range for Wednesday.(i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0915 GMT [Dow Jones] Julius Baer expects EUR/CHF to be slightly below 1.30 by the end of 2Q, with the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  likely to follow any interest rate increase from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The step-by-step resolution of the eurozone debt crisis [may] limit the safe-haven support for the franc in Q2," according to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst David Kohl. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  backdrop in 2Q should be dominated by anticipated and realised \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action in the global process of rate normalisation," he says. EUR/CHF at 1.3083 by 0913 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0912 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian shares end 0.2% lower at 1553.07 as investors book profits after recent gains, a local dealer says. OSK Research says, "it is likely that the index will start to consolidate before resuming its mission to re-test the historic peak at 1577 points." It tips immediate support at 1547 followed by another strong support at the 1531-1536 level. The dealer adds that construction stocks continue to attract demand as investors expect more new projects to be awarded by the government in coming days. The construction sub-index ends up 0.7%. Market breadth is negative with 492 losers compared with 348 gainers. Genting Malaysia (4715.KU) ends down 3.2% at MYR3.68, RHB Capital (1066.KU) closes 1.4% lower at MYR8.58 and Axiata (6888.KU) ends down 0.8% at MYR4.82. Among gainers, \uc2 Berjaya Food Bhd\uc2  (5196.KU) closes up 38% at MYR1.14, \uc2 Multi-Purpose Holdings\uc2  (3859.KU) ends up 3.4% at MYR2.76 and \uc2 Malaysian Airline System\uc2  (3786.KU) closes up 1.6% at MYR1.86. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0851 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is a tad higher late as some banks cover short-USD positions as the EUR weakened following \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgrade of Portugal's long-term government bond rating, say traders. The pair is at 43.35 from 43.31 late Monday amid heavy trading. A local dealer says the USD/PHP may continue to move higher Wednesday as the market focuses on how the EUR will react during U.S. trade to Portugal's bond rating downgrade; next resistance is tipped at 43.50 with support at 43.35. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0850 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys are treading water in London, with trading confined to narrow ranges. The ISM non-manufacturing report is out at 1400 GMT while minutes of the previous meeting of the FOMC are published at 1800 GMT. Speakers include Minneapolis Fed's Kocherlakota and Treasury Secretary Geithner. The 2-year note is unchanged at 99-31+/32 to yield 0.752%. The 10-year note is steady at 101-23+/32, yielding 3.417%. The 30-year bond is up at 104-15/32 to yield 4.477%. June bunds are unchanged at 121.17 and June gilts are down 0.10 at 116.83. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0834 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts fall after stronger than expected UK services PMI data. The services PMI for March prints at 57.1 vs expectations for a reading of 52.6. June gilts are down 0.20 at 116.73. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0824 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD has shown minimal reaction to the run of March Services PMIs which culminating with the final euro zone print being revised up to a 3 1/2 year high of 57.2 from the 56.9 flash. EUR/USD now trades at 1.4182 from 1.4185 when the Spanish PMI hit the wires almost one hour ago. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0822 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/MYR is a tad higher at 3.0270 vs 3.0254 in late Asian trade Monday, tracking a slightly weaker SGD, a local dealer says. He adds the 3.0250 level is a strong support as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been suspected of buying dollars at that level over the past few sessions. The pair is expected to continue tracking the SGD in the absence of any local leads. The dealer tips 3.0250-3.0300 range for Wednesday, and activity is likely to be thin as traders will likely be sidelined as Bank Negara is set to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the pair near the 3.0250 support. (ankur.reliaQ@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0813 GMT [Dow Jones] The willingness of Poland's Monetary Policy Council to tighten policy is boosted by an April inflation expectation reading, which jumped to 4.6% year-on-year from 3.2% in March, says Citi Handlowy. The ratio of inflation expectations to lagged current inflation increased to 1.2, which is the highest outcome since January 2008, when there was still high demand pressure in the economy, Citi says. A similar level was recorded also in September 2010, when the government announced the introduction of VAT tax increases starting from 2011. Bigger ratios were recorded only before Poland joined the EU, when Polish consumers made heavy purchases a few months before EU entry amid concerns over increases in prices. (marynia.kruk@dowjones.com) [ 04-05-11 0543ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110405e7450005f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
