\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Thai Shares End +1.6%; 1070-1080 Resistance Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,698 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2011\par 11:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0955 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares end +1.6% at 1064.35, the highest level since October 1996; volume is at its highest in four months as foreign investors continue to pour into the market, driving up heavyweight energy and banking stocks. "If you look at Thai economic data, it remains very good. Consumption and local investment keeps going very strong. So I think fund flows will keep flooding into the Thai market and the big caps will take the lead for another week," says \uc2 Kim Eng Securities\uc2  strategist Mayuree Chowvikran. Resistance is tipped at 1070-1080, with support at 1050. Among the most actively traded stocks, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) is +6.1% at THB157, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) is +4.6% at THB113, PTT Aromatics & Refining (PTTAR.TH) is +6.7% at THB39.75, \uc2 Charoen Pokphand Foods\uc2  (CPF.TH) is +1.9% at THB26.25, Banpu (BANPU.TH) is +1.3% at THB772 and \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) is +2.5% at THB184.50. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0937 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex is off 0.1% at 19,435.25 after trading in a narrow 19,390-19,450 range amid profit-taking in some large-cap shares. "A slight cool-off after eight days of gains is healthy and the market will now be looking at the (January-April) quarterly results for fundamental cues," says a local analyst. However, high crude oil prices are once again becoming a concern, he adds. ICE Brent crude May futures are up 0.1% at $117.20/bbl. Losses are led by banks, with \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2  (500112.BY) down 2.0% at INR2,713.20 and ICICI Bank (532174.BY) 0.9% lower at INR1,103.00. \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) is 0.8% lower at INR1,039.65. Among gainers, \uc2 Bharat Heavy Electricals\uc2  (500103.BY) is +2.8% at INR2,117.55. Dow Jones technical analysis tips the Sensex in a 19,230-19,575 range. (sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0932 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia rupiah-denominated government bonds continue their broad-based rise for the third session; traders tip the mini-rally to continue next week after March CPI data came in below consensus. "With the fuel subsidy rationing plan being postponed indefinitely, there's no short term risk in terms of inflation; also Bank Indonesia seems comfortable in using rupiah appreciation to combat inflation, so we don't see an imminent rate hike," says a bond trader at local securities firm. The 5-year yield falls to 7.43% from 7.54% late Thursday, the 10-year yield is a tad lower at 8.00% from 8.01%, the 15-year drops to 8.67% from 8.75%, and the 20-year falls to 9.20% from 9.26%. March CPI rose 6.65% on-year, compared with +6.84% in February, and expectations of +6.94%, while it fell 0.32% on-month compared with February's +0.13%, and expectations for a 0.07% fall. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0925 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares end up 0.8% at 3707.49 in moderate volume, led by buying in most bank and consumer-related stocks following benign March inflation data, traders say. They expect the market to continue to gain Monday on hopes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep its key interest rate unchanged at 6.75% at its monthly meeting on April 12 and tip the main index to top 3720. The index is up 0.1% since the start of the year and 7.6% since its year-low on Jan. 21. "Foreign buying continued to drive the main index higher amid hopes for higher dividend payouts," says a trader with Reliance Securities. Foreigners are net buyers of IDR625 billion worth of shares. Among gainers, Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) rose 1.7% to IDR5,850, BCA (BBCA.JK) gained 2.2% to IDR7,100, while car maker \uc2 Astra International\uc2  (ASII.JK) added 1.6% to IDR57,900. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0924 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds are barely changed in uninspiring trade. Players shrug off the March CPI data as they have already priced in the acceleration. A local dealer says yields are likely to be in a tight range trade next week as Thailand is approaching the long holiday for traditional new year celebrations. Thailand March CPI rises 3.14% on-year, while core CPI increases 1.62%. The bid/offer yield of bonds due December 2015 are quoted at 3.36%/3.34% from 3.34%/3.32% late Thursday, the October 2017 yield is at 3.525%/3.515% from 3.51%/3.50% and the December 2021 yield is at 3.72%/3.71% from 3.72%/3.70%.(piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0921 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is lower at 29.3200 from 29.4180 late Thursday on continuous foreign inflows and exporter selling, two local traders says, adding the pair also reflects stronger Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the greenback. The pair dipped to 29.2400 before the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the last 15 minutes in the onshore trading session, they say. One of the traders notes selling from exporters increased when the pair fell below 29.300 in the early afternoon. "It's difficult to predict the U.S. dollar's trading range against the New Taiwan dollar for next week due to Taiwan's upcoming four-day weekend but the greenback is likely to test 29.000 if other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Korean won remain strong," one of the traders says, tipping a 29.000-29.500 range for next week. Foreign investors were net buyers of NT$12.7 billion worth of local shares this week. (jenny.hsu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0916 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is lower at 8,697 from 8,705 late Thursday as lower-than-expected inflation in March sparks capital inflows into local stocks and bonds, dealers say. But three dealers suspect Bank Indonesia bought a total $120 million around 8,700 via state banks to slow the pair's slide. Without specifically confirming the suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Nasution tells reporters the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't want the dollar to fall too quickly to the 8,600 level from 8,700 levels. His statement indicates the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will tolerate a stronger IDR to help contain imported inflation. Dealers tip a 8,690-8,710 band for the pair Monday. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0909 GMT [Dow Jones] Spanish bond spreads have outperformed the wider euro-zone market in the last few months as positive sentiment towards the country and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  demand for bonds has seen spreads contract. To take advantage of this spread compression, Nomura suggest selling the 10-year Spain against buying 10-year Belgium, with the latter expected to gain strength when a new government is formed shortly. Nomura also points out that with Portugal likely to tap the EFSF imminently, Spain's spreads "will likely need to adjust wider." (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0907 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI ends up 0.7% at 1555.38 in an eleventh hour surge after the index traded within a tight four-point range for most of the session. A local dealer says the market was lifted as participants bought blue chips and oil and gas related stocks, and pushed even higher late after European bourses opened. "The market was mostly in consolidation mode, but sentiment remains positive... if the index manages to stay above 1550 next week, we can expect it to test 1576 (historic high) in the near-term," the dealer says. Advancers beat decliners 516 to 319. Among gainers, Genting Malaysia (4715.KU) ends up 4.3% at MYR3.84, CIMB (1023.KU) ends up 1.7% at MYR8.34, \uc2 SapuraCrest\uc2  (8575.KU) ends up 2.9% at MYR3.87 and Dialog (7277.KU) ends up 4.7% at MYR2.43. Among decliners, \uc2 Tenaga Nasional\uc2  (5347.KU) ends down 1.1% at MYR6.18 and Tan Chong (4405.KU) ends down 1.2% at MYR4.80. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0859 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS are flat, as investors shrugged off two strong PMIs, as they came in mostly in line with expectations. One-year IRS are steady at 3.29%-3.37% and five-year IRS are down 1 bp at 4.05%-4.12%. "The IRS are trading in small ranges today, and trading is rather thin before a major holiday," says a Shanghai-based local bank trader. The country's financial markets will be closed Monday and Tuesday due to Tomb Sweeping Day. China's official PMI rose to 53.4 in March from 52.2 in February, while \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's China PMI pushed up slightly to 51.8 in March from 51.7 in February. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0858 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are down on the day but stable after Portugal gets decent demand at a bond sale, albeit at higher yields. However, general consensus was that buyers had already been found for the supply so the smooth passage is not really a surprise. June bunds are down 0.37 at 120.91. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0857 GMT [Dow Jones] US Treasurys have traded with a weak tone Friday, continuing Thursday's selloff on hawkish talk from the Fed's Kocherlakota who hinted at rate increases in 2011 combined with a bullish outlook on the economy. The June contract is 12 ticks lower at 118.210, and the yield on the 10-year cash 2 bps higher at 3.485%. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  note for the 10-year to break decisively above 3.50%, Friday's nonfarm payroll figure (1230 GMT) will have to be 225,000 or higher. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com) [ 04-01-11 0555ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022 