\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Hong Kong's 2011 CPI Likely +4.7% - BEA\b0\par\par\uc2 1,463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 11:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1003 GMT [Dow Jones] Hong Kong's headline consumer price index rose 3.7% in February on-year compared to January's 3.6% increase, mainly due to the rise in private rents. February's CPI was also above the 3.2% median forecast by seven economists in a Dow Jones poll. Economists say the city's inflation will remain on an uptrend in coming months. "The effect of the higher rents have just started to emerge, there is still a long way to go for the private rents to rise more," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of East Asia\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Paul Tang. He expects CPI to rise 4.7% in 2011, more than government's forecast for a 4.5% increase. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0956 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD appears to be losing some of its upside momentum, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . With no further action from the BOJ, and a positive outcome from the upcoming E.U. summit already priced in, it's not clear what will drive the spot above 1.4280 resistance, it adds. Looking ahead, the bank says any more hawkish comments from ECB officials will be discounted as the comments of potential candidates for the top job, while an April rate hike is already largely factored in. Portugal's woes are likely to end with it falling into the arms of the EFSF before next month's elections, while data-wise Friday's German IFO is likely to show a sharp fall in expectations, all of which are EUR/USD negatives says the bank. EUR/USD now at 1.4228. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0956 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB is lower late at 30.23 vs 30.29-30.30 Monday in tandem with softer USD/Regionals, says a Bangkok-based dealer, as exporters continue to unload USD/THB, while some interbank players start to pile up short USD positions. But the pair is off a 30.21 session low on suspected buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the 30.21-30.25 level, two dealers say. "The dollar/baht looks kind of heavy. With the current sentiment (eased concerns over Japan's nuclear issues), I think the pair is bound to drift lower tomorrow," one dealer says. But any possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will limit downside. Next support is pegged at 30.20, with resistance at 30.25. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0952 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia's five-year government bonds are lower as players sought cheaper notes in the finance ministry's auction held earlier today. "The ministry only took IDR1.5 trillion from IDR4.5 trillion worth of bids for the five-year bond. Expect some of those who did not win bids to enter the secondary market Wednesday," a local trader says. He adds trading is light as players opt for the auction instead of the secondary market. The five-year yield is higher at 7.95% from 7.84% late Monday, the 10-year yield is flat at 8.21%, the 15-year yield is down at 9.07% from 9.13%, the 20-year yield is up at 9.49% from 9.48%. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0943 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts fall to session lows after CPI rises more than forecast while figures also show the government borrowed more than forecast in February. Consumer prices rose 4.4% on year in February vs expectations for a reading of 4.3%. Separately, figures show public sector net borrowing rose GBP11.8 billion vs a forecast of GBP6.9 billion. June gilts are down 0.56 at 117.81. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0940 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PKR is lower Tuesday as exporters' selling in the greenback outweighed bids by importers, a state-run bank dealer says. The pair is at 85.40 vs 85.52 late Monday. But the USD/PKR is unable to break a narrow trading band on equal buy-sell pressure, he says. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets will be closed for a national holiday Wednesday. The dealer tips the pair in a 85.40-85.50 band Thursday. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0939 GMT [Dow Jones] Sterling is higher, but not by a great deal after a mixed run of UK data. February's CPI print came in at 4.4% against the 4.3% year-year forecast, but nowhere near the speculated 4.7% that circulated earlier in the session. The PSNB came in at GBP10.3 billion, the highest February total on record while PSNCR was close to + GBP 7 billion instead of the -GBP4 billion expected. GBP/USD traded up to a fresh 14-month high of 1.6375 from around 1.6360 while EUR/GBP dipped to the day's low of 0.8696 from 0.8710. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0935 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds are mixed, with trade very sluggish as the market grasps for fresh leads to spur activity, says a Bangkok-based dealer; she adds, there was light selling from local banks on mid-range tenors, while offshore players are largely sidelined. "They (offshore players) haven't been very active this week, and I wouldn't expect them to come back in the next few days unless there are cues," she says. Yields are expected to remain around these levels Wednesday. The bid/offer yield of bonds due December 2015 are quoted at 3.30%/3.28% from 3.29%/3.23% late Monday, the October 2017 yield is at 3.53%/3.51% from 3.54%/3.51%, and the December 2021 yield is at 3.75%/3.72% from 3.75%/3.71%. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0931 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is lower at 8,710 from 8,730 late Monday, tracking the USD's weakness against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but off its lows of 8,705 on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders say. "Unlike yesterday, today we saw the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  entering the market to halt rupiah's rapid rise," says a local dealer. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  yesterday despite the pair breaching the support level of 8,750. Two dealers say the pair could reach 8,680 Wednesday should it breach the 8,700 support level. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0929 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korean refiners are expected to process more crude oil in April than in March because of higher Asian refining margins, and as a result of the magnitude-9.0 earthquake and tsunami taking offline almost a quarter of Japan's refining capacity. They plan to run about 2.485 million barrels a day of crude in April, about 45,000 barrels a day more than this month, a Dow Jones Newswires refinery survey shows. South Korea's complex refining margin to benchmark Dubai crude rose to $9.78 a barrel in the week to March 17, up $2.06 from $7.72 a barrel a month earlier, according to Dow Jones Newswires calculations. See REFINERY WATCH: S Korea April Runs Up; Japan Imports, High Margins (wayne.ma@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0924 GMT [Dow Jones] The dollar was mostly lower early in European trading Tuesday, while the euro gained ground after Monday's agreement by E.U. finance ministers on the details of a EUR500B rescue fund to become operative in July 2013. Elsewhere, sterling hit its highest level against the dollar since Jan 2010 ahead of key U.K. inflation data due at 0930 GMT. EUR/USD up at 1.4239, USD/JPY up at 81.077, GBP/USD up at 1.6327. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0924 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares ended flat at 3517.721, with selling in most commodity-related stocks offsetting gains in select blue chip banks, traders say. They expect the market to consolidate tomorrow amid an absence of fresh leads, and lingering concerns over the situation in Libya. "I think the main index may move in a narrow range of 3500-3550 tomorrow reflecting investors' caution," says a fund manager with a local securities firm. Foreigners were net sellers of IDR227 billion worth of shares. Among decliners: coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK) ended down 1.6% at IDR3,025, rival Adaro (ADRO.JK) ended down 4.3% at IDR2,225, while nickel miner Inco (INCO.JK) ended down 1.1% at IDR4,650. Bucking the trend, Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) finished up 2.0% at IDR5,200 on hopes for strong 2010 earnings to be announced later this week.(edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com) [ 03-22-11 0603ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110322e73m0007y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
