\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Lower; May Breach 8,700 Support Wed - Traders\b0\par\par\uc2 1,469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 10:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0931 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is lower at 8,710 from 8,730 late Monday, tracking the USD's weakness against regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but off its lows of 8,705 on suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders say. "Unlike yesterday, today we saw the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  entering the market to halt rupiah's rapid rise," says a local dealer. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  didn't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  yesterday despite the pair breaching the support level of 8,750. Two dealers say the pair could reach 8,680 Wednesday should it breach the 8,700 support level. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0929 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korean refiners are expected to process more crude oil in April than in March because of higher Asian refining margins, and as a result of the magnitude-9.0 earthquake and tsunami taking offline almost a quarter of Japan's refining capacity. They plan to run about 2.485 million barrels a day of crude in April, about 45,000 barrels a day more than this month, a Dow Jones Newswires refinery survey shows. South Korea's complex refining margin to benchmark Dubai crude rose to $9.78 a barrel in the week to March 17, up $2.06 from $7.72 a barrel a month earlier, according to Dow Jones Newswires calculations. See REFINERY WATCH: S Korea April Runs Up; Japan Imports, High Margins (wayne.ma@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0924 GMT [Dow Jones] The dollar was mostly lower early in European trading Tuesday, while the euro gained ground after Monday's agreement by E.U. finance ministers on the details of a EUR500B rescue fund to become operative in July 2013. Elsewhere, sterling hit its highest level against the dollar since Jan 2010 ahead of key U.K. inflation data due at 0930 GMT. EUR/USD up at 1.4239, USD/JPY up at 81.077, GBP/USD up at 1.6327. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0924 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares ended flat at 3517.721, with selling in most commodity-related stocks offsetting gains in select blue chip banks, traders say. They expect the market to consolidate tomorrow amid an absence of fresh leads, and lingering concerns over the situation in Libya. "I think the main index may move in a narrow range of 3500-3550 tomorrow reflecting investors' caution," says a fund manager with a local securities firm. Foreigners were net sellers of IDR227 billion worth of shares. Among decliners: coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK) ended down 1.6% at IDR3,025, rival Adaro (ADRO.JK) ended down 4.3% at IDR2,225, while nickel miner Inco (INCO.JK) ended down 1.1% at IDR4,650. Bucking the trend, Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) finished up 2.0% at IDR5,200 on hopes for strong 2010 earnings to be announced later this week.(edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0920 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex extends its early gains and is now up 0.7% at 17,960.26 tracking advances in most Asian markets and is supported by a thaw in crude prices. "The softening in brent crude and gains in the rupee have helped the Sensex hold at higher levels but the market is still largely range-bound," says D.D. Sharma, head of equity research at Anand Rathi Securities. Sensex gains are led by \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY), up 1.0% at INR998.00 on a media report the company is seeking an increase in the price of the gas it pumps from the KG-D6 basin. \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2  (500112.BY) is up 1.5% at INR2,622.05 while Maruti Suzuki (532500.BY) is up 3.1% at INR1,168.00 after the company says it sees no impact from the Japan crisis. Among losers, \uc2 HDFC Bank\uc2  (500180.BY) is down 0.4% at INR2,144.80. The Sensex is tipped to trade in a 17,950-18,160 band for the rest of the session.(sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0920 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD is likely to test the 1.4285-1.4295 region before topside failure sets in and a slide back toward the rising support line at 1.3970 is seen says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . Now at 1.4239. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0913 GMT [Dow Jones] Pakistan shares are higher, led by oil and gas plays. The benchmark KSE-100 index is up 1.1% at 11,503.91. "The index recovered following substantial declines in share values on Monday, enticing local and foreign investors to place orders," a dealer says. He adds, some bargain hunting by overseas funds is also providing shares with support today. Note, foreign funds sold about $26 million worth shares over the previous six sessions. He says "with several scrips having lost around 10%, fresh buying is being witnessed and hopes that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep interest rates unchanged at 14% at its monetary policy meeting Saturday is also helping sentiment." He tips the index to face resistance at 11,650 as the law and order situation in Karachi remains a worry. \uc2 Pakistan State Oil\uc2  (PSO.KA) is up 1.8% at PKR275.20, while Oil & Gas Development (OGDC.KA) is up 1.2% at PKR137.49. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0912 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian shares ends flat with the benchmark KLCI up 0.22 point at 1509.10. A local dealer says the market has been rangebound for most of the session, with some buying interest in battered-down lower-liners. Maybank IB Research technical analyst Lee Cheng Hooi expects more range-trade near-term; "some semblance of stability has returned, with the CCI, MACD and Stochastic turning positive recently," but Lee expects the market to fall in the medium-term if a key support at 1474 is breached. Market breadth is positive with 518 advancers against 250 decliners. Gainers include \uc2 Sime Darby\uc2  (4197.KU) which ends up 1.0% at MYR9.12, DRB-Hicom (1619.KU) ends 3.9% higher at MYR2.11, and \uc2 Sunway City\uc2  (6289.KU) ends up 4.0% at MYR4.42. Decliners include MISC (3816.KU) which ends 4.2% lower at MYR7.60, and F&N (3689.KU) which ends down 1.3% at MYR15.60. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0902 GMT [Dow Jones] HUF remains trading strong vs EUR as USD weakens against EUR, an Equilor analyst says. EUR/HUF is stable around 270.90, which is also its late Monday level. Equilor recommends remaining on the sidelines of the HUF market to see which direction the HUF breaks out of its current narrow range vs EUR. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0900 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is lower late Tuesday, tracking gains in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the EUR against the greenback, says a Manila-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. The pair slipped to a three-week low at 43.34 after settling at 43.55 late Monday; volume is heavy. "The market is also pricing in the expected rate hike by Thursday," the trader says, referring to the expected policy action by Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas' at its meeting later this week. Most economists tip the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise overnight rates by 25 basis points after holding rates at record lows since July 2009. Support is pegged at 43.00 with resistance at 43.50. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0859 GMT [Dow Jones] Peripheral euro-zone bond spreads are tighter in early trade after E.U. finance ministers Monday agreed a detailed package for a new bailout fund. In the 10-year space, Italy, Spain and Portugal are all 3 bps to 4 bps tighter, while Ireland widens 1.5 bps. The Irish government's refusal to cut its 12.5% corporate tax rate is still causing problems ahead of the European Council meeting starting Thursday, while the refusal of the main Portuguese opposition party to back the government's austerity package could force the resignation of PM Socrates. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0859 GMT [Dow Jones] The Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely revise balance of payments figures, moving some of whopping net errors and omissions to the current account, says Citi economist Piotr Kalisz. Even if just a third of net errors and omission is moved to the current account, it would mean a rise of the current account gap to 5%-6% of GDP this year, he adds. A rising C/A deficit and the worrying quality of the figures may prompt investors to ask for higher risk premiums, limiting the scale of the zloty's strengthening, he says. (marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com) [ 03-22-11 0531ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110322e73m0007l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
