\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Reverses Earlier Fall; 8,800 Cap Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,839 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 05:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR reverses earlier losses as the sharp fall on the Japanese bourse triggers outflows from other regional stocks, dealers say; the pair is at 8,785 from 8,765 late Monday, after falling to 8,750 earlier in the day. Three dealers suspect Bank Indonesia sold $100 million at 8,790 to slow the dollar's rise. "Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  likely \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  once so far today," a dealer says. A 8,760-8,800 range is tipped for the session; Indonesian shares are down 2.1%. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0430 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end down 2.3% at 468.74 as investors continue selling for a second straight session amid concerns that Japan's earthquake will have an impact on the economy as the Japanese market accounts for more than 10% of total Vietnamese exports. "Many players share concerns that the Japanese disaster will have a significant impact here because Japan has been a major donor to many infrastructure development projects here whose funding is likely to be hurt this year," says a Hanoi-based brokerage trader. Among the most actives Saigon Securities (SSI.VH) ended down 0.4% at VND23,600, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) closed down 1.4% at VND13,700 and Vietinbank (CTG.VH) finished 1.5% higher at VND27,500. Traders predict more losses Wednesday and tip support for the key index at 460. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0426 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end lower, reversing from early session gains, amid a substantial decline in other regional bourses. The benchmark PSEi ends off 0.6% at 3896.31, with 102 decliners and 35 advancers while 36 issues were unchanged. "The declines in other markets are quite huge, causing foreign selling in the local market," says Joseph Roxas, Eagle Equities president. Roxas adds the index may stay within a 3850-3950 range Wednesday as the market watches developments in Japan following last week's massive quake. Among the most active stocks Alliance Global (AGI.PH) ended down 2.0% at PHP11.80, Energy Development (EDC.PH) ended up 1.6% at PHP6.18, and Aboitiz Equity (AEV.PH) ended up 0.8% at PHP41.35. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0425 GMT [Dow Jones] A fund manager at Aberdeen International Fund Managers says the gainers from the Japan earthquake will be commodities used for reconstruction in the country, picking up some of the slack as China's economy slows. "If there are any gainers, cynically opportunistic though that may sound, they are likely to lie among resource companies such as \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) and BHP (BHP.AU). Japan will need thermal coal and iron ore to boost non-nuclear power alternatives." (nisha.gopalan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0422 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 index is down 2.7% at 4500.2 after hitting a 6-month low of 4479.7, following an intraday bounce from 4492.4 to 4544.8. Japan's worsening nuclear disaster is fueling extreme volatility amid expected global liquidation of funds by Japanese investors. Japan's Nikkei futures fell more than 16% intraday after Prime Minister Naoto Kan said that an explosion and fire at the Fukushima nuclear power plant had produced a high level of radiation with the danger of further leakage. "People holding long positions are now seeing headlines suggesting this nuclear disaster is really deteriorating," says IG Markets institutional dealer Chris Weston. "If it does blow out to a full meltdown, all hell will break loose." AUD/USD weakness continues to point to offshore selling, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diving to 0.9962 from 1.0097 this morning. The S&P/ASX 200 index is heading for the next support level of 4469.0 (61.8% retracement of the July 2010-February 2011 rise). Former support at 4451-4464 is now expected to offer resistance. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0421 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex opens sharply lower and is down 2.5% at 17,971.34, tracking negative cues from the Asian markets after news of further explosions at a nuclear power plant in Japan, which elevates the risk of a spread in radiation. Japan's Nikkei plunges 12.8%. "Investors across the globe are concerned about the collateral damage caused by the crisis situation in Japan," a local analyst says; he adds, "investors are also worried about crude oil prices shooting up again as they view going ahead, there can be an increase in crude oil imports in Japan with disruption in its nuclear generation capacity." But he expects some value buying toward the later half of the trading session in banking and metal stocks; he tips the Sensex in a 17,900-18,350 range intraday. The Sensex's losses are led by autos and metal stocks. Maruti Suzuki (532500.BY) is down 4.0% at INR1,208.50, \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) is down 3.6% at INR579.30, \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) is down 2.4% at INR994.10, \uc2 Infosys Technologies\uc2  (500209.BY) is down 2.6% at INR3,020 and ICICI Bank (532174.BY) is down INR2.5% at INR994. (swagata.gupta@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0421 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP extends its gains as worries over the latest explosion in Japan's troubled nuclear power plant undermines regional bourses and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The pair is at 43.86 from 43.62 earlier today, and after settling at 43.59 late Monday. Traders peg support at 43.50 and resistance at 44.00. "Renewed concerns over Japan has triggered another round of risk aversion. We might see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  start to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  later today to calm the market," a local bank trader says. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0414 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/INR is near a three-week high as local stocks open deeply negative on souring risk sentiment as Japan struggles to grapple with a nuclear emergency following radiation leaks from a reactor, says a trader at a state-run bank. The pair is at 45.35 after quickly touching a high of 45.37 in early trading, compared with 45.07 late Monday. The Sensex is down 2.6%. "The pair rose sharply in a wild start and seems to stabilizing now, but a further upside to the dollar cannot be ruled out as the threat of a radiation leak spreading across Southeast Asia lurks," he says. He tips the pair in a 45.25-45.45 band for the session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0411 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is down 3.8% at 22,449.60 midday, although it has recovered from an intraday low of 22,253.47; market volume at half day spikes to HK$60.95 billion, almost matching HK$65.1 billion all of Monday. "Market sentiment is definitely panicky," according to Alvin Cheung, associate director at Prudential Brokerage, as investors are spooked by concerns over a radiation leak at a Japanese nuclear plant. Still, Cheung adds some bargain-hunting has emerged near the 22,200 level, which may act as an intraday support. "There is no need for Hong Kong investors to over-panic, given what I believe is an undemanding valuation for local stocks," Cheung says; he estimates the HSI is trading at a forward 2011 P/E of 13.7X. But the Nikkei's post-midday-break plunge may spur further selling in the local market; the Nikkei is down 14.4%. All 45 blue chips are down. Among the top three losers, Ping An (2318.HK) tumbles 6.7% to HK$76.25 after its share placement, \uc2 Hang Lung Properties\uc2  (0101.HK) falls 6.6% to HK$30.55 and Cosco Pacific (1199.HK) is down 6.3% at HK$14.24. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0410 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares surrender their early gains, tracking the sharp decline in Japan and other regional markets after the latest explosion at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear plant. The benchmark PSEi is off 0.5% at 3899.97 in moderate volume as decliners beat gainers 105 to 30. Support for the PSEi is pegged at 3850, with resistance at 3950. "I guess we can no longer ignore the losses we're seeing around the region, particularly the Nikkei," says Jomar Lacson, research head at Campos Lunaza and Co. Leading losers are Alliance Global Group (AGI.PH), down 2.0% to PHP11.80 and \uc2 DMCI Holdings\uc2  (DMC.PH), down 0.5% to PHP37.75, and Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH), down 2.8% at PHP60.30--all of which were gainers earlier in the trading day. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0405 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is down 13% at 8,365.20, falling below 8,500--its lowest intraday level since April 2009--following a double-digit plunge in futures during the midday break on news of a fire at \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2 's (9501.TO) No. 4 reactor at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant in northeastern Japan. Nikkei 225 June futures are now off 17% at 7,820, while Topix futures have been halted at 774.50. Reports said radiation has spiked near the No. 3 reactor. "It's panic selling. It's not only the foreign investors, but everybody just wants to dump shares," says Retela Crea Securities general manager Yosuke Shimizu, putting the Nikkei's next support level at 8,000. All 33 Topix subindexes are down. \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2 , or "Tepco", remains limit-down, ask-only at an indicative price of Y1,221. \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) is down 7.2% at Y2,873, \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7201.TO) is off 6.9% at Y672. Construction shares, which had enjoyed a surge of buying on Monday on hopes for reconstruction demand in the earthquake and tsunami-affected regions, are also sharply lower, off 15% as a group; general contractor \uc2 Kajima Corp\uc2 . (1812.TO) is down 16% at Y217. \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) is off 13% at Y2,228. The Topix index is off 14% at 730.76. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0403 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead June JGB futures are sharply higher, rising more than Y1 on-day in the afternoon session, as the Nikkei plunges over 13%; the contract hit 141.17--the highest level since Dec. 8. The benchmark 10-year cash JGB yield could break below the 1.1% mark this week (vs 1.150% currently) as it's hard to predict how the crisis at the crippled nuclear power plants will end up, boosting demand for safe-haven assets, says Deutsche Securities strategist Makoto Yamashita. Also, "there is a risk that, even if the government tries to provide fiscal help (on post-quake reconstruction), production activities and sentiment (among firms and households) will deteriorate sharply," he adds. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com) [ 03-15-11 0035ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110315e73f0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal600
