\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK: Predicted Ranges, Themes-2-\b0\par\par\uc2 924 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 02:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 USD/PHP biased lower. It would make sense for the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 'at least take a first step toward raising policy rates' by 25 bps March 24, 'even though it may chose to wait-and-watch after that,' says \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  economist Devika Mehndiratta, adding that Gov. Tetangco''''s comment that the scope to keep rates low has narrowed 'does signal to us a possible shift in this direction.' A trader at a local bank said Tetangco''''s 'comments are turning positive' toward a hike, but that 'the situation in the Middle East may continue to cause jitters.' He tips pair USD/PHP falling toward 43.30 support today with the next resistance is 43.70. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate resistance is at 44.75 (Jan. 18 high), then 45.00 (round figure) and 45.58 (Aug. 16 peak). Support at 43.00 (big figure), then 42.34 (Nov. 4 low).\par \par \uc2 USD/CNY downward bias. China is likely to increase banks'reserve requirement ratio soon to curb excess liquidity, state-run China Securities Journal says in a commentary, after the latest data showed a pick-up in foreign capital inflows into the country in January. The longer-term trend in the USD/CNY though is still subject to a little debate with ING economist Tim Condon saying, 'we no longer expect the authorities to restore a CNY appreciation trend in the second half of the year;' the bank raised its one-year USD/CNY forecast target to 6.40 from 6.35, citing Beijing's pro-growth bias. A Shanghai-based local bank trader tips the pair to consolidate around Wednesday's fixing at 6.5736, saying, 'we expect the yuan to remain rangebound for the next two weeks while Beijing is pushing out the next five-year plan.' Dow Jones technical analysis shows USD/CNY 1-year NDF consolidating in the 6.5560-6.3840 range; NDF was last at 6.4120 vs spot at 6.5713. Spot USD/CNY has immediate support at 6.5417 (trendline from Oct. 15 low), below at 6.5140 (bottom edge of trend channel from Dec. 20 high). Initial resistance at 6.5850 (trendline from Dec. 20 high), before 6.6017 (Jan. 31 high), then 6.6077 (55-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 USD/THB biased downwards. The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  apparently bought USD/THB around 30.54-30.55 Wednesday, says a Bangkok dealer: 'It may have swallowed as much as US$1 billion ... but its effort is considered effective as the pair is almost frozen after it stepped in.' The traders expect firm support at 30.50 to limit any downside, while resistance is tipped at 30.60 near-term. Equity inflows could see the pair test yesterday''''s \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels again as expectations that authorities will allow gradual appreciation is still the underlying assumption given that the oil price remains high. Dow Jones technical analysis shows USD/THB has immediate resistance at 31.19 (Jan. 31 high), then 32.00 (July 20 low, bottom of previous base). Support at 30.32 (Jan. 19 low), before 30.00 (big figure) then 29.50 (Nov. 20 low).\par \par \uc2 USD/MYR mild downward bias. A local dealer says, 'the trend is still for a stronger ringgit, but the rise will more likely be gradual,' tipping a 3.0330-3.0500 range Thursday. Equities in Asia should see a much better day after Wednesday''''s falls and Malaysia should be pulled along given the absence of local drivers. Expectations of a strong January exports number on Friday should lend a mild bullish bias at the periphery. The USD/SGD''''s approach of all-time lows should leave the USD/MYR with a heavy downside bias. A local dealer tips the pair to trade in a 3.0330-3.0500 range today. Dow Jones technical analysis shows initial support at 3.0220 (Feb.21 low), then 3.0000 (big figure). Initial resistance is at 3.0695 (Jan. 21 peak), before 3.0830 (Jan. 10 high).\par \par \uc2 USD/IDR range bound. Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are over $100 billion, enough to overcome any USD/IDR "volatility," says Bank Indonesia Gov. Nasution, adding that IDR is still in a strengthening trend. This could also mean BI is satisfied with the buffer it has and can afford to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less on USD/IDR declines. \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to be one of the few Asian emerging markets still enjoying strong consistent inflows so BI actually has a policy choice. This is especially so given the impending roadshow on March 17 for its global bond auction; expectations of a good auction of around $1.5 billion will weigh on the pair over the medium term. Given the recent positive remarks from all the ratings agencies, expect spreads on Indonesian sovereigns to continue to narrow in anticipation of a good sale. For the near term, Friday's BI policy meeting will retain the market's focus, with a local dealer saying, "I think they want to see Bank Indonesia's policy before deciding to have more exposure," adding current sentiment toward emerging markets remains fragile. The pair is tipped in a 8,800-8,830 range Thursday. Dow Jones technical analysis shows USD/IDR trading sideways in the short-term; initial resistance is at 9,069 (Jan. 18 peak), above at 9,088 (Jan. 11 peak); support is at 8,800 (psychological), then 8,640 (May 2007 low).\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110304e733001b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
