\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Indonesia Raises Interest Rates Amid Inflation Concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alex Frangos  \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL  \par \uc2 1,000 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2011\par 10:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted interest rates in the face of increasing inflation and investor agitation, highlighting the difficult position the world's emerging markets face in managing growth.\par \par \uc2 Southeast Asia's largest economy has boomed in recent years as political stability, low debt and economic overhauls have attracted a flood of foreign money. While that inflow makes inexpensive capital available for investment, it also complicates the country's economic management and illustrates the inherent dangers of capital flows on emerging economies generally.\par \par \uc2 Those dangers were on display in the lead-up to Friday's rate decision to raise its target rate to 6.75% from a record low 6.5%, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first move since August 2009. Foreign investors, spooked by rising inflation, sold $1 billion of Indonesian government bonds in a two-week period last month, sending bond-market interest rates sharply higher, the stock market down 11% at one point, and increased pressure on authorities to quell fears of an inflation spiral.\par \par \uc2 "Markets are waking up to the fact that the monetary authorities have this very delicate balancing act, and they could get it badly wrong," said Vijay Chander, fixed-income analyst at Standard Chartered in Hong Kong. "If they get behind curve on hiking rates, then inflation rips and rates are soaring out of control."\par \par \uc2 The influx of investor cash into emerging markets since the financial crisis ebbed has preoccupied policy makers from Brazil to South Korea. They worry ultra-low interest rates in the U.S., Europe and Japan will overwhelm their faster growing economies. Several nations and the International Monetary Fund have advocated the use of limited capital controls, or limits on money flows across borders. Indonesia, for example, last year imposed holding periods on some types of investments.\par \par \uc2 While inflows are generally seen as a positive sign for an economy, short-term investments can leave as quickly as they come in, whipsawing financial markets and causing wider economic damage.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia is one example. Global investors unsatisfied with the low interest rates in the developed world have bought $9.5 billion in Indonesian government bonds since the start of 2010. That has helped double the foreign-ownership share of Indonesian government to 30% from 15%.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's ability to manage inflation and capital flows will have consequences for the country's ability to break into the top echelon of powerhouse emerging markets: Brazil, China, India and Russia. As a Group of 20 member, Indonesia's experience is likely to be closely watched by others.\par \par \uc2 Not wanting too much of that inflow, Indonesia has resisted raising interest rates, fearing higher rates would attract even more investors, putting pressure on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise. Last year, \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  routinely in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, selling rupiah for U.S. dollars to prevent its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising too quickly and making its products less competitive, especially compared with China's. It has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  so heavily, several analysts think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will need an infusion of capital from the government to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheet.\par \par \uc2 The foreign capital built up in Indonesia's financial system has created a vulnerability. If that money flows out quickly, interest rates increase too rapidly for local banks and businesses to manage. And it puts pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken. A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exacerbates inflation by making imported goods, especially foods and energy products, even more expensive for local businesses and consumers.\par \par \uc2 Driven by rising food prices, Indonesia's inflation rate jumped in November and December to 7%, above the bank's target of 5% plus-or-minus one percentage point.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia Gov. Darmin Nasution dismissed inflation concerns in an interview Monday. "We do not consider we are behind the curve yet," he said, noting much of the inflation pressure is in volatile food prices that he expects to wane. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said "macroprudential" tools such as raising banking reserve requirements and targeting specific asset prices was sufficient.\par \par \uc2 Investors have been less confident. In early January, money began pouring out of the country as investors worried the value of their investments would be eroded by inflation. Interest rates on 10-year government bonds went from 7.5% at the start of the year to as high as 10% on Jan. 21. They have since settled around 9%. Improved economic prospects in the U.S. and Europe have also caused investors to reorient their holdings toward developed markets and away from emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 The outflows put pressure on the Indonesian rupiah to weaken against the dollar, and sparked the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to draw on its $96 billion in foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to prevent the rupiah from falling.\par \par \uc2 "They were able to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under control by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  quite strongly," said Philippe Petit, who manages $17 billion in emerging-market bonds for Pictet Funds in Singapore. He thinks the selling is over for now. "The big investors haven't run," he said.\par \par \uc2 The mostly Muslim nation's strong domestic economy helped it weather the global downturn in trade and manufacturing. Moody's Investors Service upgraded Indonesia's debt rating at the start of January recognizing the progress the country has made since the Asian financial crisis in the late 1990s. Indonesia, with the world's fourth largest population--240 million people--grew a healthy 6% last year, but still lags behind the 10% growth in China and nearly 9% growth in India.\par \par \uc2 "Indonesia has a once-in-a-generation chance to substantially accelerate economic growth," said Manu Bhaskaran, partner in Singapore for Centennial Group, an economic research firm. "It would be a pity if all the good work done was negated because monetary policy was not tightened enough."\par \par \uc2 -By Alex Frangos, The Wall Street Journal; alex.frangos@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 --Natasha Brereton contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 04, 2011 04:58 ET (09:58 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110204e724001sw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
