\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Loses Steam; 9,030-9,070 Tipped Next Wk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,363 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2011\par 10:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0949 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR loses steam, last at 9,040 vs intraday high of 9,080 and 9,068 late yesterday, on suspected Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and late USD selling, dealers say. "The pair was steady around the 9,065-9,070 level until late dollar selling from foreigners and locals, who are likely buying stocks on dip," a local dealer says. But dealers say Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  isn't likely to be \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily. "We suspected them buying small amounts in the morning and around midday." Three dealers suspect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to have sold around $10 million at IDR9,075 via state banks in the morning and around $30 million at IDR9,080 midday to stem the pair's rise. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0947 GMT [Dow Jones] If EUR/USD breaks above 1.3625 shorts should "cut and run," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 , citing a selloff in German bund markets. Says the last time German/US two-year yields widened so much, EUR/USD was trading at 1.42. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says while 1.3625 resistance holds, it retains a bearish view. EUR/USD currently at 1.3539. (eva.szalay@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0946 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds are mixed in thin trade, with some buying interest by local funds in medium term notes, says a local trader. Volume is light with only a handful of transactions being executed as most players hold off amid the absence of fresh cues, she says. The market shrugs off \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's flagging of concerns over accelerating inflation and higher risks that core inflation may reach the upper-end of its 0.5%-3.0% target range this year. "These comments have hardly had any impact on the market at all as most participants have already anticipated further hikes anyway. Besides, the next policy meeting (March 9) is still sometime away," says the trader. Yields are expected to remain within a tight range early next week. The bid/offer yields of issues due December 2015 are quoted at 3.22%/3.19% from 3.22%/3.18% late yesterday, while October 2017 yields are quoted at 3.52%/3.47% from 3.50%/3.45% and June 2019 yields are at 3.58%/3.48% from 3.60%/3.50%. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0946 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungary's fiscal policy poses a risk to inflation, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  says. While the government should be able to book a 6%-of-GDP budget surplus in 2011 thanks to a transfer of pension assets to the public sector, this will only be a one-off event, Citi adds. Introducing a flat-rate income tax and increasing taxes on selected sectors are likely to boost inflation in 2011. The government planning to extend extraordinary sectoral taxes beyond 2012 is a large source of uncertainty for the business sector and thus may be a drag on investment growth. Additionally, the bank tax is to limit banking sector profits, curb credit growth and thus may slow recovery. (margit.feher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0937 GMT [Dow Jones] There has only been a small fall in sterling despite December retail sales coming in at -0.8% on the month, twice as bad as expected. GBP/USD sheds just 15 ticks to 1.5890, EUR/GBP trades at 0.8623 from 0.8516 prior the numbers.(gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0935 GMT [Dow Jones] US Treasurys are trading in positive territory in London with the 10-year future up at 120.030 after hitting a low Thursday of 119.220. Thursday's weakness was sparked-off by better than anticipated initial jobless claims, home sales and leading indicators data. Later auction announcements came out in-line with expectations. In the US cash market, the 2-year yield is at 0.61% and the 5-year at yield 2.015%, both 4.5 bps tighter, while the 10-year is currently 2.5 bps tighter at 3.423%. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0932 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB marches higher to 30.65-30.66 from 30.55 late yesterday on portfolio outflows following consistent foreign selling of Thai shares in recent sessions; they have been net sellers worth THB17.54 billion between Jan. 4 to Jan. 20. The pair is biased up in the next week due to month-end demand from corporates, says a Bangkok-based dealer. "Importers' demand (to buy USD) also emerges for fear of further strength in the U.S. dollar, while exporters are waiting for a higher level to place their sales orders," she adds. Resistance is pegged at 30.80 and support is at 30.60 Monday. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0930 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PKR is off highs at 85.91 late after rising to session high of 86.07 vs 85.81 late Thursday. A dealer says dollar demand from importers was high; "a struggling economy and widening trade deficit are also exerting pressure on the Pakistan rupee," he says. The July-December trade deficit widened 18% to $8.15 billion from a year earlier. However, sales by exporters cashing in on high dollar levels helped the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he adds. Tips the pair in 85.95-86.05 range Monday. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0920 GMT [Dow Jones] The Japanese government's Friday estimate that its annual budget gap could hit Y23.2 trillion in fiscal 2021 is "not a surprising at all," and "it just reinforced that Japan needs to act quicker in reforming fiscal health," says Takahira Ogawa, director of sovereign ratings at \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2 . Still, he doubts the current administration has the ability to do so, especially as the passage of any bills is seen difficult in the coming diet session due to the controversial appointment of Economy Minister Kaoru Yosano. "The government needs a better strategy to swiftly pass bills though the diet. Without it, any good bills would just end up being a pie in the sky," he says (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0917 GMT [Dow Jones] A UK bank trader is hearing chatter of good-sized EUR/USD stops above 1.3575, and says with the euro looking bid across the board it's probably just a matter of time until these are taken out. EUR/USD now at 1.3552 from the day's 8-week high of 1.3569. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0913 GMT [Dow Jones] HUF is relatively stable, after Thursday's high volatility, as stock markets in Europe are in positive territory again. The EUR/HUF is at 275 versus Thursday's sharp rise from 272.20 to 265.50. Takarekbank expects the cross to trade in the 273-276 range for the rest of the day, with some HUF strengthening anticipated, especially before Monday's expected 25bp Hungarian rate increase. Raiffeisen Bank agrees that the HUF's strengthening streak is unbroken but notes that Thursday's high volatility reflects how vulnerable the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still is. (margit.feher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0912 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds extend losses in the afternoon session and are at their day's low, pressured by a INR110 billion government paper supply scheduled later in the day and ahead of the RBI's rate-setting meeting on Tuesday, a dealer at a state-run bank says. The 8.13% 2022 bond is at the session low of 99.39, compared with 99.65 at Thursday's close. "Most traders are cutting their position ahead of the RBI policy meet; we can see the bearish trend continuing till the end of the session," the dealer says. He tips the yield of 8.13% 2022 bonds in a 8.19%-8.23% band for the session (currently 8.21%). Today's auction includes INR40 billion of 7.99% 2017 bonds, INR40 billion of 8.08% 2022 bonds and INR30 billion of the 8.26% 2027 bonds. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com) [ 01-21-11 0449ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110121e71l0005y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
