\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: China Home Prices To Rise At Slower Pace - Fitch\b0\par\par\uc2 1,608 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2011\par 03:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] China's primary-market home prices will continue to rise this year but at a slower rate, Fitch Ratings says. "China's residential property sector will be supported by favorable near-term global and domestic macroeconomic conditions, underpinned by a persistent global liquidity glut and the potential appreciation of the Chinese yuan," says Ying Wang, a director at Fitch's Asia-Pacific Corporates. The average selling price of homes in tier 1 cities rose 20%-40% on-year, while the average selling price rose 6.0% nationwide in 2010, says Fitch. It adds that China's planned real estate tax will "only have a marginal impact on property prices and considers full-fledged property taxes on a national scale to be a remote possibility." Fitch says that its rated Chinese property developers have low refinancing risks since most have already refinanced their 2011 and 2012 bond maturities. While tightened bank lending will continue, mid-sized and large developers still have access to project loans, Fitch says. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares rise 1.4% to 519.22 led by financial stocks on a strong economic outlook, and also as investors buy on hopes for more solid 4Q earnings after blue chip stock FPT Corp (FPT.VH) says its net profit rose 18.8% on-year to VND1.263 trillion in 2010. "Sentiment continues to improve because the state media is still reporting a positive economic outlook for the country this year after the Party Congress recently appointed a new leadership," a Hanoi-based brokerage analyst says. Among advancers, \uc2 Bao Viet Holdings\uc2  (BVH.VH) is up 4.0% at VND103,000, Vietcombank (VCB.VH) is 4.8% higher at VND33,000 and Vietinbank (CTG.VH) climbs 5.0% to VND23,100. FPT is up 1.6% at VND64,500. The analyst tips immediate resistance at 522. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0222 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is at 29.126, up from a trading band of 29.025-29.050 yesterday, as some foreign investors start to buy amid falling local stocks, says a local trader. "Some foreign investors found that the New Taiwan dollar has strong resistance at NT$29.000 against the U.S. dollar...and they don't want to park their money here too long, especially as the Lunar New Year holidays are approaching." He adds the pair's direction is likely to be affected by the performance of local stocks in the near term (the Taiex is last down 0.8%). He tips the pair to trade in a 29.100-29.150 band for the session. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0222 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by one-month at-the-money USD/JPY options are unchanged from late Thursday in New York at 9.70%/10.40% as the USD/JPY remains in a narrow 82.86-83.09 range in spot trading, says a senior dealer at a major Japanese bank. He expects the pair to be rangebound for the rest of the session, fenced in by exporters' selling and speculators' buying. He adds, volatilities are likely to start falling as investors may start selling toward the weekend. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0222 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude oil futures are a tad lower in early Asia trade on a bearish inventory report late Thursday and amid concerns of further monetary policy tightening by China; although previous measures by Beijing haven't slowed oil demand, interest rate hikes have had a S/T bearish impact on crude; a report that another hike may be only weeks away will weigh on sentiment. Weaker regional shares and a stronger USD are likely to limit buying interest today, maintaining downward pressure. March Nymex is down 2 cents at $89.57/bbl on Globex; ICE Brent is down 14 cents at $96.44/bbl. (gurdeep.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares are down 0.9% at 8941.63, weighed by declines in the U.S. tech-heavy Nasdaq overnight, says Concord Securities analyst Allan Lin. He adds, media reports that the government is cracking down on illegal stock investment companies yesterday also weigh on sentiment. "With the benchmark index rising above the 9000 level (recently), most investors are wary of any bad news, and prefer to sell their stocks (to lock in profits) before the upcoming Lunar New Year holidays." But he adds, the rebound in China stock markets today as well as some local tech shares such as \uc2 Largan Precision\uc2  (3008.TW) is providing some support. He tips the Taiex to find initial support at its 30-day moving average around 8930, with next support at 8900; he adds, resistance is pegged at 9000. TSMC (2330.TW) is down 1.8% at NT$74.90, UMC (2303.TW) is down 0.6% at NT$17.55, and \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) is down 1.7% at NT$28.60. \uc2 Largan Precision\uc2  is up 0.4% at NT$774. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW is lurching in and out of gains amid a lack of any strong one-sided momentum; the pair is nearly flat at 1,121.50 from 1,121.20 late Thursday in Seoul. Traders note upward momentum for the pair isn't very strong despite concerns over further monetary policy tightening by China ahead of the weekend; "I don't see the kind of (strong) short dollar covering by offshore players we saw yesterday," helping the pair retreat a bit, says a local bank trader, who expects the pair to test 1,120 support. Exporters aren't selling much in the morning session, but if they sell USD in bigger volumes in the afternoon, the pair may slip below 1,120. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0213 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is falling as banks start to unload some long USD positions. The pair is last quoted at 44.42 in moderate volume after settling late yesterday at 44.50 and spiking to 44.61 earlier today. Traders peg support at 44.30. "It seems a lot of banks were caught long, so they're easing up on those positions. We haven't seen the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far," says a local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY is higher, tracking gains in the USD index. Friday's central parity is set at 6.5886, slightly higher than Thursday's record low of 6.5883, indicating that the yuan's rally in the run up to the Hu-Obama summit meeting may have ended, a Shanghai-based European bank trader says. On OTC, the USD/CNY is at 6.5883 from 6.5854 at Thursday's close; resistance is tipped at 6.5950. "President Hu's visit to the U.S. doesn't appear to have offered anything new on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tensions," the trader adds. Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY NDFs are unchanged from 6.4760/6.4800 late Thursday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals are up on some decent dip buying, after a second day of weakness in London. LME 3-month copper is at $9,405.75/ton, up $50.75 with 1,163 lots done and zinc is at $2,353/ton, up $23 with 970 lots done. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says that despite rising inventories and what is describes as a "lull" in the physical market, investors should look at the bigger picture and not lose faith in the medium-term bullish outlook. "This is an eerie replication of market conditions early last year and our warning to market participants is the same now as it was then: don't be fooled," it says in a report. The bank says the pullback is "a healthy correction after the market got a bit ahead of itself." (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares are likely to fall, weighed by lingering inflation concerns and a weak rupiah, traders say. They expect the main index to bottom at 3440 after it ended down 2.3% at 3454.118 Thursday. "I expect that selling pressure today won't be as heavy as yesterday as many institutional investors will likely start to accumulate shares of companies that would report strong 2010 earnings," says a fund manager at a local state-owned company. Commodity-related stocks such as coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK) and palm oil company Astra Agro (AALI.JK) may lead falls on lower oil prices, but Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) may rebound on bargain buying after Thursday's 3.7% fall. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is well bid on lingering concerns over domestic inflation risks; however, suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is preventing the pair from rising sharply, dealers say. The pair is at 9,070, from 9,068 late yesterday; three dealers suspect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to have sold around $10 million at IDR9,075 via state banks. "We are increasingly cautious about the direction of Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and debt markets as there is a risk that Bank Indonesia might fall behind the policy curve as inflationary pressure threatens to gain further traction," says Bank Of America \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 . Dealers tip the pair in a 9,065-9,085 range for the session. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com) [ 01-20-11 2127ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110121e71l0001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
