\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Indonesia Shares Set To Fall; 3440 Support Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2011\par 03:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares are likely to fall, weighed by lingering inflation concerns and a weak rupiah, traders say. They expect the main index to bottom at 3440 after it ended down 2.3% at 3454.118 Thursday. "I expect that selling pressure today won't be as heavy as yesterday as many institutional investors will likely start to accumulate shares of companies that would report strong 2010 earnings," says a fund manager at a local state-owned company. Commodity-related stocks such as coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK) and palm oil company Astra Agro (AALI.JK) may lead falls on lower oil prices, but Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) may rebound on bargain buying after Thursday's 3.7% fall. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is well bid on lingering concerns over domestic inflation risks; however, suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is preventing the pair from rising sharply, dealers say. The pair is at 9,070, from 9,068 late yesterday; three dealers suspect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to have sold around $10 million at IDR9,075 via state banks. "We are increasingly cautious about the direction of Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and debt markets as there is a risk that Bank Indonesia might fall behind the policy curve as inflationary pressure threatens to gain further traction," says Bank Of America \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 . Dealers tip the pair in a 9,065-9,085 range for the session. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] The state-run China Securities Journal's commentary saying that China's CPI could exceed 6.0% in part of the first half of the year "is shocking, but not completely unrealistic," says Soichiro Mori, a senior strategist at FXOnline Japan. China's CPI eases to +4.6% in December from +5.1% in November, but that's probably due to "a brief weakening in consumer spending due to cold weather in winter," he says, adding that as China goes into the Lunar New Year holidays, consumption may accelerate, which will likely result in a higher CPI. Currently, the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market hasn't factored in such a potential surge in Chinese prices, but once the forecasts are digested, "the market may see some wild swings," he says. Tokyo traders say if expectations for a more hawkish stance from China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  increase, the USD and the EUR may fall, while the JPY is to benefit due to its safe-haven status. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are higher on bargain hunting after yesterday's falls, but thin trade shows sentiment remains cautious, which will curb further upside. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.2% at 2682.81, with analysts tipping 2700 as immediate resistance. "The market is still in a downward trend, as all eyes are on whether China can keep inflation in check," says Capital Securities analyst Li Bin, citing the increasing likelihood of 5% CPI growth in 1Q11. Heavyweight banks and property developers rise after their recent falls, with ICBC (601398.SH) up 0.5% at CNY4.17 after Thursday's 1.9% fall, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (601939.SH) up 0.2% at CNY4.67 after falling 2.3% yesterday, property developer Gemdale (600383.SH) +0.7% to CNY6.80 after a 4.0% fall yesterday and Poly Real Estate (600048.SH) up 1.3% to CNY13.20 after a 6.3% fall. The Shenzhen Index is up 0.2% at 1172.45. (amy.li@dowj