emerging market positions and going long U.S. equities again." For the STI, it says short-term RSI conditions are currently oversold due to yesterday's sharp sell down; it also says minor resistance is at 3213 before 3220, a rejection of which "would be very bearish." Support at 3190/3185 then 3170. ST Engineering (S63.SG) climbs 1.5% to S$3.34 on contract news and \uc2 Mapletree Logistics Trust\uc2  (M44U.SG) rises 1.0% to S$0.99 post-4Q results. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB rises to 30.60 from 30.55 late yesterday on buying by gold traders and offshore players. Portfolio outflows following the consistent foreign selling of Thai shares in recent sessions are also supporting the USD/THB, says Bangkok-based dealer. "It looks more heavy on the buy side while many exporters are holding up, waiting for a better level to place their sale orders," she says. Immediate resistance is pegged at 30.65 while support at 30.55 will likely hold for the day. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] The CHF is a "dual performer," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  strategist Calvin Tse says, because "it has properties to outperform in a risk-off scenario, but it can also hold its own in a pro-risk environment." As investor concerns about European debt problems linger, the CHF remains a "promising" safe-haven, he adds. During a period of risk seeking, "the Swiss economy looks poised to continue its recovery, with leading indicators near their highs," he notes. The EUR/CHF is at 1.3033 vs 1.3036 late Thursday and the USD/CHF is at 0.9682 vs 0.9676.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares may continue to drift lower. The benchmark PSEi may slide toward immediate support at 3970 after closing at a two-month low of 4006.24 Thursday, which extended its decline to a third day and increased its losses to 3.4%. "Definitely we've entered a corrective phase, but the sentiment isn't bearish. Investors are unloading stocks they bought at the highs to preserve capital," says Jomar Lacson, research head at Campos Lanuza & Co. "2011 is unexciting. The government isn't spending much and you don't have the boost from last year's elections. We'll probably see the correction peppered with small rallies, with strong support (for PSEi) at 3900." Profit-taking on recent gainers is tipped to continue, with likely targets including \uc2 Aboitiz Power\uc2  (AP.PH), Alliance Global Group (AGI.PH) and \uc2 Manila Electric\uc2  (MER.PH). PSEi resistance is pegged at 4050. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is down 0.9% at 1552.60 in early trade in tandem with most regional markets as a policy tightening move by China appears more than likely in the near term. "Market sentiment is very weak across the region at the moment. We're also catching up to yesterday's regional sell off (as Malaysian markets were closed for a holiday Thursday), so the KLCI will probably fall more than other regional markets today," a local trader says. He tips immediate support at the key 1550 psychological level. The market breadth is negative with losers outnumbering gainers by over 5 to 1. Genting Malaysia (4715.KU) is down 2.3% at MYR3.42, \uc2 Petronas Chemicals\uc2  (5183.KU) is down 2.4% at MYR6.20 and \uc2 Axiata Group\uc2  (6888.KU) is down 2.2% at MYR4.78. \uc2 Tenaga Nasional\uc2  (5347.KU) is down 0.6% at MYR6.45 on weak 1Q results and bleak FY11 guidance. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0114 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD is "the best of the G3" \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  strategist Calvin Tse due to growing optimism on the U.S. economic outlook. "We expect the U.S. economy to grow at 3.6% this year, compared to 1.5% and 1.2% for the euro zone and Japan respectively." The EUR is being supported by some investors' view that the ECB may hike rates by the 3rd quarter of this year, but Tse says "that looks overdone. As rate expectations are scaled back, likely due to another flare up in peripheral worries, we believe the euro will correct lower." The USD/JPY is at 83.01, the EUR/USD is at 1.3464, and the EUR/JPY is at 111.75. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com) [ 01-20-11 2110ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110121e71l0001e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
