\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Crude Tad Lower; Weak Shares, Strong USD To Weigh\b0\par\par\uc2 1,648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2011\par 03:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0222 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude oil futures are a tad lower in early Asia trade on a bearish inventory report late Thursday and amid concerns of further monetary policy tightening by China; although previous measures by Beijing haven't slowed oil demand, interest rate hikes have had a S/T bearish impact on crude; a report that another hike may be only weeks away will weigh on sentiment. Weaker regional shares and a stronger USD are likely to limit buying interest today, maintaining downward pressure. March Nymex is down 2 cents at $89.57/bbl on Globex; ICE Brent is down 14 cents at $96.44/bbl. (gurdeep.singh@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares are down 0.9% at 8941.63, weighed by declines in the U.S. tech-heavy Nasdaq overnight, says Concord Securities analyst Allan Lin. He adds, media reports that the government is cracking down on illegal stock investment companies yesterday also weigh on sentiment. "With the benchmark index rising above the 9000 level (recently), most investors are wary of any bad news, and prefer to sell their stocks (to lock in profits) before the upcoming Lunar New Year holidays." But he adds, the rebound in China stock markets today as well as some local tech shares such as \uc2 Largan Precision\uc2  (3008.TW) is providing some support. He tips the Taiex to find initial support at its 30-day moving average around 8930, with next support at 8900; he adds, resistance is pegged at 9000. TSMC (2330.TW) is down 1.8% at NT$74.90, UMC (2303.TW) is down 0.6% at NT$17.55, and \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) is down 1.7% at NT$28.60. \uc2 Largan Precision\uc2  is up 0.4% at NT$774. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW is lurching in and out of gains amid a lack of any strong one-sided momentum; the pair is nearly flat at 1,121.50 from 1,121.20 late Thursday in Seoul. Traders note upward momentum for the pair isn't very strong despite concerns over further monetary policy tightening by China ahead of the weekend; "I don't see the kind of (strong) short dollar covering by offshore players we saw yesterday," helping the pair retreat a bit, says a local bank trader, who expects the pair to test 1,120 support. Exporters aren't selling much in the morning session, but if they sell USD in bigger volumes in the afternoon, the pair may slip below 1,120. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0213 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is falling as banks start to unload some long USD positions. The pair is last quoted at 44.42 in moderate volume after settling late yesterday at 44.50 and spiking to 44.61 earlier today. Traders peg support at 44.30. "It seems a lot of banks were caught long, so they're easing up on those positions. We haven't seen the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far," says a local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY is higher, tracking gains in the USD index. Friday's central parity is set at 6.5886, slightly higher than Thursday's record low of 6.5883, indicating that the yuan's rally in the run up to the Hu-Obama summit meeting may have ended, a Shanghai-based European bank trader says. On OTC, the USD/CNY is at 6.5883 from 6.5854 at Thursday's close; resistance is tipped at 6.5950. "President Hu's visit to the U.S. doesn't appear to have offered anything new on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tensions," the trader adds. Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY NDFs are unchanged from 6.4760/6.4800 late Thursday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals are up on some decent dip buying, after a second day of weakness in London. LME 3-month copper is at $9,405.75/ton, up $50.75 with 1,163 lots done and zinc is at $2,353/ton, up $23 with 970 lots done. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says that despite rising inventories and what is describes as a "lull" in the physical market, investors should look at the bigger picture and not lose faith in the medium-term bullish outlook. "This is an eerie replication of market conditions early last year and our warning to market participants is the same now as it was then: don't be fooled," it says in a report. The bank says the pullback is "a healthy correction after the market got a bit ahead of itself." (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares are likely to fall, weighed by lingering inflation concerns and a weak rupiah, traders say. They expect the main index to bottom at 3440 after it ended down 2.3% at 3454.118 Thursday. "I expect that selling pressure today won't be as heavy as yesterday as many institutional investors will likely start to accumulate shares of companies that would report strong 2010 earnings," says a fund manager at a local state-owned company. Commodity-related stocks such as coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK) and palm oil company Astra Agro (AALI.JK) may lead falls on lower oil prices, but Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) may rebound on bargain buying after Thursday's 3.7% fall. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is well bid on lingering concerns over domestic inflation risks; however, suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is preventing the pair from rising sharply, dealers say. The pair is at 9,070, from 9,068 late yesterday; three dealers suspect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to have sold around $10 million at IDR9,075 via state banks. "We are increasingly cautious about the direction of Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and debt markets as there is a risk that Bank Indonesia might fall behind the policy curve as inflationary pressure threatens to gain further traction," says Bank Of America \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 . Dealers tip the pair in a 9,065-9,085 range for the session. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] The state-run China Securities Journal's commentary saying that China's CPI could exceed 6.0% in part of the first half of the year "is shocking, but not completely unrealistic," says Soichiro Mori, a senior strategist at FXOnline Japan. China's CPI eases to +4.6% in December from +5.1% in November, but that's probably due to "a brief weakening in consumer spending due to cold weather in winter," he says, adding that as China goes into the Lunar New Year holidays, consumption may accelerate, which will likely result in a higher CPI. Currently, the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market hasn't factored in such a potential surge in Chinese prices, but once the forecasts are digested, "the market may see some wild swings," he says. Tokyo traders say if expectations for a more hawkish stance from China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  increase, the USD and the EUR may fall, while the JPY is to benefit due to its safe-haven status. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are higher on bargain hunting after yesterday's falls, but thin trade shows sentiment remains cautious, which will curb further upside. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.2% at 2682.81, with analysts tipping 2700 as immediate resistance. "The market is still in a downward trend, as all eyes are on whether China can keep inflation in check," says Capital Securities analyst Li Bin, citing the increasing likelihood of 5% CPI growth in 1Q11. Heavyweight banks and property developers rise after their recent falls, with ICBC (601398.SH) up 0.5% at CNY4.17 after Thursday's 1.9% fall, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (601939.SH) up 0.2% at CNY4.67 after falling 2.3% yesterday, property developer Gemdale (600383.SH) +0.7% to CNY6.80 after a 4.0% fall yesterday and Poly Real Estate (600048.SH) up 1.3% to CNY13.20 after a 6.3% fall. The Shenzhen Index is up 0.2% at 1172.45. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0153 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD edges up to 7.7849 from 7.7817 late yesterday, but it's unlikely to rise steeply as the buoyant economy is still luring fund inflows, says a trader at a local bank. "The U.S. dollar/Hong Kong dollar forward contracts show support for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Thus, the recent correction in local stock market and the Hong Kong dollar is only temporary." He tips the pair to trade between 7.7830 and 7.7860 band today. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] China's first interest rate hike in 2011 could come around the Lunar New Year holiday in early February, state-run China Securities Journal said in a commentary Friday without citing sources. The paper said that strong inflation pressure could push the consumer price index to exceed 6.0% during part of 1H. The paper also said Beijing will continue to raise the reserve requirement ratio for banks, and allow the yuan to appreciate to help lower the price of imported goods. China Securities Journal is a paper designated for disclosing securities news under the Xinhua News Agency instead of through the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Although the paper didn't cite any sources, the report comes at a time when investors are concerned that China's inflation could pick up further. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 20, 2011 21:22 ET (02:22 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110121e71l000eu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
