\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Well Bid; 9,065-9,085 Range Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,748 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2011\par 03:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is well bid on lingering concerns over domestic inflation risks; however, suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is preventing the pair from rising sharply, dealers say. The pair is at 9,070, from 9,068 late yesterday; three dealers suspect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to have sold around $10 million at IDR9,075 via state banks. "We are increasingly cautious about the direction of Indonesia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and debt markets as there is a risk that Bank Indonesia might fall behind the policy curve as inflationary pressure threatens to gain further traction," says Bank Of America \f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Dealers tip the pair in a 9,065-9,085 range for the session. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] The state-run China Securities Journal's commentary saying that China's CPI could exceed 6.0% in part of the first half of the year "is shocking, but not completely unrealistic," says Soichiro Mori, a senior strategist at FXOnline Japan. China's CPI eases to +4.6% in December from +5.1% in November, but that's probably due to "a brief weakening in consumer spending due to cold weather in winter," he says, adding that as China goes into the Lunar New Year holidays, consumption may accelerate, which will likely result in a higher CPI. Currently, the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market hasn't factored in such a potential surge in Chinese prices, but once the forecasts are digested, "the market may see some wild swings," he says. Tokyo traders say if expectations for a more hawkish stance from China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  increase, the USD and the EUR may fall, while the JPY is to benefit due to its safe-haven status. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are higher on bargain hunting after yesterday's falls, but thin trade shows sentiment remains cautious, which will curb further upside. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.2% at 2682.81, with analysts tipping 2700 as immediate resistance. "The market is still in a downward trend, as all eyes are on whether China can keep inflation in check," says Capital Securities analyst Li Bin, citing the increasing likelihood of 5% CPI growth in 1Q11. Heavyweight banks and property developers rise after their recent falls, with ICBC (601398.SH) up 0.5% at CNY4.17 after Thursday's 1.9% fall, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (601939.SH) up 0.2% at CNY4.67 after falling 2.3% yesterday, property developer Gemdale (600383.SH) +0.7% to CNY6.80 after a 4.0% fall yesterday and Poly Real Estate (600048.SH) up 1.3% to CNY13.20 after a 6.3% fall. The Shenzhen Index is up 0.2% at 1172.45. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0153 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD edges up to 7.7849 from 7.7817 late yesterday, but it's unlikely to rise steeply as the buoyant economy is still luring fund inflows, says a trader at a local bank. "The U.S. dollar/Hong Kong dollar forward contracts show support for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Thus, the recent correction in local stock market and the Hong Kong dollar is only temporary." He tips the pair to trade between 7.7830 and 7.7860 band today. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] China's first interest rate hike in 2011 could come around the Lunar New Year holiday in early February, state-run China Securities Journal said in a commentary Friday without citing sources. The paper said that strong inflation pressure could push the consumer price index to exceed 6.0% during part of 1H. The paper also said Beijing will continue to raise the reserve requirement ratio for banks, and allow the yuan to appreciate to help lower the price of imported goods. China Securities Journal is a paper designated for disclosing securities news under the Xinhua News Agency instead of through the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Although the paper didn't cite any sources, the report comes at a time when investors are concerned that China's inflation could pick up further. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0144 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's STI rises 0.1% to 3208.47 after a weaker open, with bearish regional cues tussling with a resilient Wall Street session Thursday and also on some buying back after yesterday's 1.1% drop. STI components are evenly mixed, with declines in banks and gains in property names. The broad market is also evenly matched, with 244 million shares traded worth S$196 million. After overnight losses for crude oil and copper, Phillip Securities says the commodity market "is suggesting risk-off at this point" and that it might be "further evidence of unwinding of emerging market positions and going long U.S. equities again." For the STI, it says short-term RSI conditions are currently oversold due to yesterday's sharp sell down; it also says minor resistance is at 3213 before 3220, a rejection of which "would be very bearish." Support at 3190/3185 then 3170. ST Engineering (S63.SG) climbs 1.5% to S$3.34 on contract news and \uc2 Mapletree Logistics Trust\uc2  (M44U.SG) rises 1.0% to S$0.99 post-4Q results. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB rises to 30.60 from 30.55 late yesterday on buying by gold traders and offshore players. Portfolio outflows following the consistent foreign selling of Thai shares in recent sessions are also supporting the USD/THB, says Bangkok-based dealer. "It looks more heavy on the buy side while many exporters are holding up, waiting for a better level to place their sale orders," she says. Immediate resistance is pegged at 30.65 while support at 30.55 will likely hold for the day. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] The CHF is a "dual performer," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  strategist Calvin Tse says, because "it has properties to outperform in a risk-off scenario, but it can also hold its own in a pro-risk environment." As investor concerns about European debt problems linger, the CHF remains a "promising" safe-haven, he adds. During a period of risk seeking, "the Swiss economy looks poised to continue its recovery, with leading indicators near their highs," he notes. The EUR/CHF is at 1.3033 vs 1.3036 late Thursday and the USD/CHF is at 0.9682 vs 0.9676.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares may continue to drift lower. The benchmark PSEi may slide toward immediate support at 3970 after closing at a two-month low of 4006.24 Thursday, which extended its decline to a third day and increased its losses to 3.4%. "Definitely we've entered a corrective phase, but the sentiment isn't bearish. Investors are unloading stocks they bought at the highs to preserve capital," says Jomar Lacson, research head at Campos Lanuza & Co. "2011 is unexciting. The government isn't spending much and you don't have the boost from last year's elections. We'll probably see the correction peppered with small rallies, with strong support (for PSEi) at 3900." Profit-taking on recent gainers is tipped to continue, with likely targets including \uc2 Aboitiz Power\uc2  (AP.PH), Alliance Global Group (AGI.PH) and \uc2 Manila Electric\uc2  (MER.PH). PSEi resistance is pegged at 4050. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is down 0.9% at 1552.60 in early trade in tandem with most regional markets as a policy tightening move by China appears more than likely in the near term. "Market sentiment is very weak across the region at the moment. We're also catching up to yesterday's regional sell off (as Malaysian markets were closed for a holiday Thursday), so the KLCI will probably fall more than other regional markets today," a local trader says. He tips immediate support at the key 1550 psychological level. The market breadth is negative with losers outnumbering gainers by over 5 to 1. Genting Malaysia (4715.KU) is down 2.3% at MYR3.42, \uc2 Petronas Chemicals\uc2  (5183.KU) is down 2.4% at MYR6.20 and \uc2 Axiata Group\uc2  (6888.KU) is down 2.2% at MYR4.78. \uc2 Tenaga Nasional\uc2  (5347.KU) is down 0.6% at MYR6.45 on weak 1Q results and bleak FY11 guidance. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0114 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD is "the best of the G3" \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  strategist Calvin Tse due to growing optimism on the U.S. economic outlook. "We expect the U.S. economy to grow at 3.6% this year, compared to 1.5% and 1.2% for the euro zone and Japan respectively." The EUR is being supported by some investors' view that the ECB may hike rates by the 3rd quarter of this year, but Tse says "that looks overdone. As rate expectations are scaled back, likely due to another flare up in peripheral worries, we believe the euro will correct lower." The USD/JPY is at 83.01, the EUR/USD is at 1.3464, and the EUR/JPY is at 111.75. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is down 0.7% at 4748.3 after hitting a 6-day low of 4744.4 on the China Securities Journal editorial warning that China could hike interest rates around Chinese New Year, which begins Feb. 3. The last rate hike from China occurred on Christmas day, so there's some logic to the idea of China hiking using Chinese New Year to cushion the blow to financial market sentiment from another rate hike. On the charts, today's break of the 20-day moving average line at 4760 leaves the index headed for the uptrend line from the Dec. 1 low, which comes in near 4728, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. That line could form support from a potential bullish triangle pattern, after the false break above key resistance at 4815.0 on Wednesday. If 4728 fails to hold, the focus should turn to 4705, which is the weekly uptrend line from July 2010. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com) [ 01-20-11 2108ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110121e71l0001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
